WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.996 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.996 --> 00:00:03.992 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.992 --> 00:00:05.489 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.489 --> 00:00:10.978 align:middle line:90%


00:00:10.978 --> 00:00:12.490 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Sorry.

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:16.127 align:middle line:90%
So here's syntax five.

00:00:16.127 --> 00:00:17.710 align:middle line:84%
If you're wondering
whether we're ever

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:21.550 align:middle line:84%
going to stop doing syntax,
we are, but I'm not sure when.

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:25.900 align:middle line:84%
I think we have at least one
or two more days of syntax.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.910 align:middle line:84%
So if you're looking
at the syllabus

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:31.480 align:middle line:84%
to try to figure out where
we are, just don't do that.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.820 align:middle line:84%
I'll try to update
the syllabus soon

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:39.530 align:middle line:84%
to give you some idea of what's
going to happen in the future.

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:42.850 align:middle line:84%
Last time, we got
started on something

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:46.010 align:middle line:84%
that I promised you at the
beginning of the semester.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:46.510 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:48.177 align:middle line:84%
I'm just going to
close this real quick.

00:00:48.177 --> 00:00:50.225 align:middle line:90%


00:00:50.225 --> 00:00:51.850 align:middle line:84%
I said at the beginning
of the semester

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:54.225 align:middle line:84%
that one of the kinds of things
we were going to find out

00:00:54.225 --> 00:00:58.023 align:middle line:84%
was that, although there
were lots kinds of languages

00:00:58.023 --> 00:00:59.440 align:middle line:84%
out there in the
world and we were

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:02.080 align:middle line:84%
going to have a chance
to look at some of them,

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:05.700 align:middle line:84%
that one of the things that
we find as we study these--

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.700 align:middle line:84%
I turned out not to be
able to close that door--

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.940 align:middle line:90%
Let me try that again.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:13.840 align:middle line:84%
One of the things
that we find as we

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.080 align:middle line:84%
study languages of the world
is that, although there

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:18.830 align:middle line:90%
are many languages out there--

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:22.540 align:middle line:84%
oh, well-- I've been
defeated by a door.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:23.798 align:middle line:90%
This is very sad.

00:01:23.798 --> 00:01:25.840 align:middle line:84%
That although there are
many languages out there,

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.150 align:middle line:84%
there are kinds of languages
that are easy to imagine

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:30.590 align:middle line:90%
but that don't, in fact, exist.

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:35.690 align:middle line:84%
And there's a hypothesis
about what's going on,

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:39.010 align:middle line:84%
which is that part of
being a human being

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:42.370 align:middle line:84%
is having the kind of mind
that can construct language

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:43.870 align:middle line:90%
in some ways, but not others.

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:47.920 align:middle line:84%
That is, given a
certain set of data,

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:51.550 align:middle line:84%
the human mind jumps to certain
conclusions but not others

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:54.525 align:middle line:84%
about this system that's
underlying those data.

00:01:54.525 --> 00:01:56.650 align:middle line:84%
And one of the things that
we're trying to find out

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:58.990 align:middle line:84%
as we study linguistics
is about what

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:01.340 align:middle line:84%
it is about human minds that
makes them work this way.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:03.580 align:middle line:84%
What are the rules
that our minds

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:07.680 align:middle line:84%
are using to make sense of
the limited data that we have?

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.630 align:middle line:84%
We ran very quickly through
one argument of that kind

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:12.873 align:middle line:84%
last time because we
were running low on time,

00:02:12.873 --> 00:02:15.040 align:middle line:84%
and I thought, maybe we
have time for this argument.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:18.220 align:middle line:84%
And probably, looking
back, we probably didn't.

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:20.770 align:middle line:84%
If you are looking
at the-- if you

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:22.540 align:middle line:84%
get a chance to look
at the video of that,

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:24.290 align:middle line:84%
if anybody wants to,
you should look at it

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:28.400 align:middle line:84%
on slowed down speed to see if
you can get it to make sense.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.430 align:middle line:84%
So I was going to go
through that argument again

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:32.530 align:middle line:84%
and some other
arguments a little more.

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:34.150 align:middle line:84%
That's going to be
a theme for today.

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:38.390 align:middle line:84%
I will show you some areas
in which languages vary,

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:41.480 align:middle line:84%
and then we will imagine
kinds of languages,

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:44.080 align:middle line:84%
and we'll discover that those
kinds that are easy to imagine

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.160 align:middle line:90%
don't happen.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:49.330 align:middle line:90%
So we're going to look at that.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:50.770 align:middle line:90%
This is 24.900.

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:52.870 align:middle line:84%
If you go on and take
more linguistics classes,

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:55.810 align:middle line:84%
you will see more phenomena
of this kind and more theories

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:57.950 align:middle line:84%
about why languages
behave this way.

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:00.760 align:middle line:84%
But trying to understand why
languages behave this way

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:03.970 align:middle line:84%
is one of the central
occupations of linguistics,

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:07.730 align:middle line:84%
trying to understand basically
what human minds are like.

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:10.250 align:middle line:84%
So last time I showed
you the slide--

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:12.470 align:middle line:84%
this was a slide for the
question, "What will Mary

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:13.670 align:middle line:90%
write?"

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:16.340 align:middle line:84%
I was trying to
convince you that,

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:20.510 align:middle line:84%
in a sentence like this where
we think that "write" selects

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:21.230 align:middle line:90%
an object--

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:24.440 align:middle line:84%
it has the option of
being transitive--

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:26.900 align:middle line:84%
you can say Mary
is writing a novel.

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:30.140 align:middle line:84%
And we thought that when
you had a selection relation

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:32.960 align:middle line:84%
between the head
and some phrase,

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:36.220 align:middle line:84%
that the phrase needed to
be the sister to the head.

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:38.720 align:middle line:84%
At least, if there was only one
thing that it was selecting.

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:41.305 align:middle line:84%
We spent a lot of
happy time thinking

00:03:41.305 --> 00:03:42.680 align:middle line:84%
about what happens
when a head is

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:43.940 align:middle line:90%
selecting more than one thing.

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:48.410 align:middle line:84%
And I promised you that we would
talk more about that later.

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:50.600 align:middle line:84%
But that promise did
not apply to today.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:51.920 align:middle line:90%
So today, yeah.

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:53.630 align:middle line:84%
If a head is selecting
only one thing,

00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:55.250 align:middle line:84%
that thing needs
to be the sister.

00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:58.010 align:middle line:84%
And then we looked at
questions like this,

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:01.710 align:middle line:84%
and I tried to convince
you to be upset.

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:04.172 align:middle line:84%
So the word "what," which
seems to be the thing that's

00:04:04.172 --> 00:04:05.630 align:middle line:84%
being selected by
"write," it seems

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:07.010 align:middle line:90%
to be the thing that's getting--

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:09.170 align:middle line:90%
that is the object of "write"--

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:10.850 align:middle line:90%
isn't anywhere near "write."

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:12.530 align:middle line:84%
What I said was there
is a standard way

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:14.030 align:middle line:84%
to deal with this
problem, and we're

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:17.180 align:middle line:84%
going to gradually accumulate
evidence for this idea.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:20.250 align:middle line:84%
But for today, I'll
just show you the idea.

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:24.290 align:middle line:84%
The idea is that, indeed,
what does start off

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:29.390 align:middle line:84%
as the sister of "write" goes
right where the projection

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:30.800 align:middle line:90%
principle says it should.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:32.270 align:middle line:90%
It's merged right away.

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:33.920 align:middle line:84%
As soon as "write"
is introduced,

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:35.420 align:middle line:84%
first thing you
merge "write" with

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:37.700 align:middle line:84%
is something that "write"
is selecting-- its object.

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:41.660 align:middle line:84%
But then there is an operation
that obscures that, destroys

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:44.323 align:middle line:84%
that relation, takes that
thing which starts off

00:04:44.323 --> 00:04:46.490 align:middle line:84%
as the sister of "write"
and moves it to this higher

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:48.620 align:middle line:90%
position in the clause.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:52.010 align:middle line:84%
I said that it moved to
the specifying of CP.

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:54.780 align:middle line:84%
That is that position
that's there on the tree.

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:58.250 align:middle line:84%
So it becomes the
daughter of the CP node.

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:01.130 align:middle line:84%
CP, maybe you remember,
is the projection

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:03.740 align:middle line:84%
that's headed by words
like "that" and "whether"

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:07.640 align:middle line:84%
in sentences like "I think
that it is raining," talking

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:09.890 align:middle line:84%
about that as being the place
where we would put words

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:11.400 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:14.180 align:middle line:84%
Does this all sound familiar
and not terribly upsetting?

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.850 align:middle line:84%
Is there anything in any of
this that makes any of you

00:05:16.850 --> 00:05:19.370 align:middle line:84%
want to demand
further explanation

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:22.070 align:middle line:84%
or even ask nicely for
further explanation?

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:23.420 align:middle line:90%
Anything else like that?

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:23.960 align:middle line:90%
No?

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:25.980 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:30.305 align:middle line:90%
So one of the-- so wh-movement.

00:05:30.305 --> 00:05:31.680 align:middle line:84%
Cross-linguistically
very common.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:34.110 align:middle line:84%
Lots of languages out there
that have this operation

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:37.080 align:middle line:84%
that takes these wh-words,
these question words

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:39.150 align:middle line:84%
with meanings like "what"
and "who" and "where"

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:43.540 align:middle line:84%
and "why" and puts them at
the beginning of the sentence.

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:46.560 align:middle line:84%
So hearing examples from
English and Tagalog and Finnish.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:49.080 align:middle line:90%


00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:52.598 align:middle line:84%
The language that you're working
on could be a kind of thing

00:05:52.598 --> 00:05:54.390 align:middle line:84%
that you could try to
find out "How does it

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:55.590 align:middle line:90%
do its wh-questions?"

00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:58.560 align:middle line:84%
Because there is
cross-linguistic variation--

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:00.660 align:middle line:90%
we said this last time too--

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:02.680 align:middle line:84%
with respect to how you
form the wh-question.

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:04.222 align:middle line:84%
So there are languages,
like English,

00:06:04.222 --> 00:06:06.540 align:middle line:90%
which do wh-movement usually.

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:09.840 align:middle line:84%
We talked about special
conversational circumstances

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:11.940 align:middle line:84%
under which you might
leave a "wh" in-situ.

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:14.790 align:middle line:84%
So there are times when
it's possible to say

00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:17.070 align:middle line:90%
"Mary wrote what."

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:19.950 align:middle line:84%
But it specifically
has to involve either

00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:22.890 align:middle line:84%
not having heard you say
exactly what Mary wrote

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:25.200 align:middle line:90%
or being amazed.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:30.120 align:middle line:84%
So you say, "Mary wrote
an Estonian novel."

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:31.620 align:middle line:90%
And I say, "Mary wrote what?

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:35.310 align:middle line:84%
I didn't know she
spoke Estonian."

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:37.462 align:middle line:84%
But except under those
special circumstances,

00:06:37.462 --> 00:06:39.420 align:middle line:84%
the normal thing to do
with wh-words in English

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:41.420 align:middle line:84%
is to put them at the
beginning of the sentence.

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:44.160 align:middle line:84%
There are other languages,
like Mandarin or Bafut--

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.860 align:middle line:84%
which is a language of
Cameroon-- or Hopi--

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:49.290 align:middle line:84%
which is a language of
the American Southwest--

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:51.430 align:middle line:84%
many, many languages
which don't do that.

00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:53.010 align:middle line:84%
They have what's
called "wh in-situ."

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:56.640 align:middle line:84%
That is, the wh-phrases just
stay where they would normally

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:59.370 align:middle line:90%
be in the language in question.

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:03.098 align:middle line:84%
So Chinese word order is not
unlike English word order.

00:07:03.098 --> 00:07:05.640 align:middle line:84%
You've got the subject and then
the verb and then the object.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:08.735 align:middle line:84%
And the word for "what"
there is sitting right

00:07:08.735 --> 00:07:10.110 align:middle line:84%
where an object
would normally be

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:15.120 align:middle line:84%
if you wanted to say "Zhangsan
bought an Estonian novel"

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:15.870 align:middle line:90%
in Mandarin--

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:17.162 align:middle line:90%
I have no idea how to say that.

00:07:17.162 --> 00:07:19.410 align:middle line:84%
I don't speak
Mandarin well at all.

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:22.080 align:middle line:84%
The words for "Estonian novel"
would go right after "bought."

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:24.390 align:middle line:84%
It would go right
where "what" is.

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:25.860 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:28.590 align:middle line:90%
Same deal for Bafut and Hopi.

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:29.970 align:middle line:90%
Hopi word order is different.

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:31.595 align:middle line:84%
The verb is at the
end of the sentence,

00:07:31.595 --> 00:07:33.600 align:middle line:84%
but the generalization
about all these languages

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:36.000 align:middle line:84%
is that the wh-phrase is
just staying where it is.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:38.973 align:middle line:84%
There is no
WH-movement going on.

00:07:38.973 --> 00:07:40.890 align:middle line:84%
And what I said last
time-- and this is true--

00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:43.560 align:middle line:84%
is those seem to be
the options, basically.

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:46.215 align:middle line:84%
You either put your wh-phrase
at the beginning of the sentence

00:07:46.215 --> 00:07:51.120 align:middle line:84%
when you do wh-movement or
you leave it where it is.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:53.600 align:middle line:84%
You could imagine other
things that you would do.

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:55.850 align:middle line:84%
You could imagine a language
where the wh-phrase would

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:58.130 align:middle line:84%
move to the end of the
sentence or a language

00:07:58.130 --> 00:07:59.678 align:middle line:84%
where the wh-phrase
would have to be

00:07:59.678 --> 00:08:01.220 align:middle line:84%
in the middle of
the sentence-- you'd

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:05.240 align:middle line:84%
count the words in the sentence
and make sure it was evenly

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:07.100 align:middle line:90%
in the middle of the sentence.

00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:10.880 align:middle line:84%
There aren't any
languages like that.

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:12.743 align:middle line:90%
Now, so here's an example.

00:08:12.743 --> 00:08:14.660 align:middle line:84%
There are no languages
in which the normal way

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:16.370 align:middle line:84%
to say "who has
eaten the cookies" is

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:17.300 align:middle line:84%
"has eaten the
cookies who?" where

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:18.883 align:middle line:84%
I've put in all these
silly diacritics

00:08:18.883 --> 00:08:20.905 align:middle line:84%
to emphasize the fact
that I'm making this up.

00:08:20.905 --> 00:08:22.280 align:middle line:84%
There are no
languages like this.

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:23.655 align:middle line:90%
This is not a real language.

00:08:23.655 --> 00:08:25.220 align:middle line:90%
So I'm making up conlang.

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:25.735 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:08:25.735 --> 00:08:27.110 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Are
there any languages

00:08:27.110 --> 00:08:30.230 align:middle line:84%
in which [? move ?] to the
beginning of the sentence

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:35.534 align:middle line:84%
or just the in situ [INAUDIBLE]
are they both [INAUDIBLE]..

00:08:35.534 --> 00:08:36.409 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes.

00:08:36.409 --> 00:08:37.130 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:39.130 align:middle line:84%
Excellent question, and
I should have said that.

00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:41.360 align:middle line:84%
There are languages that
allow you to do either one.

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:44.030 align:middle line:84%
French is a language
like that, for example.

00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:49.012 align:middle line:84%
And, in fact, it's not uncommon
for languages like that to--

00:08:49.012 --> 00:08:50.720 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the first
thing to say about them

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:52.790 align:middle line:90%
is that they have both options.

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:55.580 align:middle line:84%
It's not uncommon for one of
the options to be more common

00:08:55.580 --> 00:08:57.650 align:middle line:90%
or to be less restricted.

00:08:57.650 --> 00:09:01.190 align:middle line:84%
So French allows you to do
wh-in-situ, but not always.

00:09:01.190 --> 00:09:03.093 align:middle line:84%
There are restrictions
on it, and people

00:09:03.093 --> 00:09:04.760 align:middle line:84%
get very interested
in the restrictions,

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:08.360 align:middle line:84%
trying to figure out when do
you have to wh-move in French.

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:09.110 align:middle line:90%
But yes.

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:11.960 align:middle line:84%
The short version of the
answer to your question is yes.

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:15.440 align:middle line:90%
Other questions about this?

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:16.390 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:09:16.390 --> 00:09:18.660 align:middle line:84%
So I started this
off by saying we're

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:20.660 align:middle line:84%
going to spend some time
talking about languages

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:21.540 align:middle line:90%
that don't exist.

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:22.850 align:middle line:84%
Here's a language
that doesn't exist.

00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:25.225 align:middle line:84%
There aren't languages that
take wh-phrases and move them

00:09:25.225 --> 00:09:27.150 align:middle line:90%
to the end of the sentence.

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:30.592 align:middle line:84%
And if you're syntactician,
you want to know, well, why?

00:09:30.592 --> 00:09:32.300 align:middle line:84%
Why aren't there any
languages like that?

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:35.540 align:middle line:90%
It's not hard to imagine them.

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:39.990 align:middle line:84%
Now, when I am
trapped on an airplane

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:42.338 align:middle line:84%
and the person next to
me wants to talk to me,

00:09:42.338 --> 00:09:44.130 align:middle line:84%
and, for some reason,
they want to continue

00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:45.510 align:middle line:84%
to talk to me after
I tell them that I'm

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:46.770 align:middle line:90%
a theoretical syntactician.

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:48.510 align:middle line:84%
If I tell them
this, if I tell them

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:51.360 align:middle line:84%
about this kind of
contrast, they sometimes

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:53.550 align:middle line:84%
respond by saying--
sometimes they say, "Oh, yes.

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:54.717 align:middle line:90%
That's very interesting...."

00:09:54.717 --> 00:09:56.580 align:middle line:84%
And then they start
reading their novel.

00:09:56.580 --> 00:09:59.850 align:middle line:84%
But sometimes they respond
by saying, yeah, but look.

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:04.560 align:middle line:84%
In a wh-question, the wh-word
is the important thing.

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:06.570 align:middle line:90%
So maybe it's just--

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:08.940 align:middle line:84%
if you ask, why aren't there
any languages like this,

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:12.930 align:middle line:84%
maybe it's just
if it's important,

00:10:12.930 --> 00:10:15.353 align:middle line:84%
then you want it to be
first, something like that.

00:10:15.353 --> 00:10:16.770 align:middle line:84%
The theory doesn't
usually get any

00:10:16.770 --> 00:10:18.395 align:middle line:90%
more sophisticated than that.

00:10:18.395 --> 00:10:20.520 align:middle line:84%
So if you're going to do
anything with a wh-phrase,

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:23.340 align:middle line:84%
maybe it's not so
surprising that it's first.

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:26.460 align:middle line:84%
And so I actually
want to show you

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:28.470 align:middle line:84%
a kind of apparent
counterexample

00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:30.870 align:middle line:90%
to what I've just claimed.

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:33.990 align:middle line:84%
Because the fact is
that there are languages

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:37.410 align:middle line:84%
that arrange for the wh-word to
be at the end of the sentence,

00:10:37.410 --> 00:10:41.380 align:middle line:84%
it's just that they are
always wh-in-situ languages.

00:10:41.380 --> 00:10:44.890 align:middle line:84%
Let me explain to you
what I mean by that.

00:10:44.890 --> 00:10:46.930 align:middle line:84%
So in order to tell you
what I mean by that,

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:48.513 align:middle line:84%
I first have to tell
you another point

00:10:48.513 --> 00:10:50.820 align:middle line:90%
of variation between languages.

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:58.440 align:middle line:84%
There are languages in which the
normal way to form wh-questions

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:02.430 align:middle line:84%
is it involves what's
sometimes called a cleft.

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:05.280 align:middle line:84%
It's as though you have
to say in these languages

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:07.380 align:middle line:84%
"What is the one
that you bought?"

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:10.170 align:middle line:84%
or What is the thing
that you bought?"

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:11.850 align:middle line:84%
Some of the languages
that are like this

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:14.350 align:middle line:84%
only do this for some of
their kinds of wh-questions.

00:11:14.350 --> 00:11:17.110 align:middle line:84%
Some of them do it for all
other kinds of wh-questions.

00:11:17.110 --> 00:11:19.380 align:middle line:84%
So these are languages in
which you can't literally

00:11:19.380 --> 00:11:20.640 align:middle line:90%
say "What did you buy?"

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:23.160 align:middle line:84%
You have to do this more
complicated structure.

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:25.740 align:middle line:84%
I'm not going to try to give
you a tree for this more

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:26.670 align:middle line:90%
complicated structure.

00:11:26.670 --> 00:11:27.907 align:middle line:90%
But something like this.

00:11:27.907 --> 00:11:30.240 align:middle line:84%
Something that involves more
than one clause, basically.

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:33.450 align:middle line:84%
So you have a "what" at the
beginning of the sentence.

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:34.830 align:middle line:84%
But inside that
sentence, there's

00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:36.630 align:middle line:84%
this other clause
"that you bought."

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:37.410 align:middle line:90%
That's a CP.

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:41.730 align:middle line:84%
Look, there's a C at the
beginning of it-- that.

00:11:41.730 --> 00:11:43.650 align:middle line:84%
In English, we can ask
questions like, "What

00:11:43.650 --> 00:11:44.817 align:middle line:90%
is the one that you bought?"

00:11:44.817 --> 00:11:46.980 align:middle line:84%
But in Tagalog, for
example, you have to.

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:49.230 align:middle line:84%
So you can't just say, "What
did you buy?" in Tagalog.

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:52.920 align:middle line:84%
You have to say, [TAGALOG]
where the [TAGALOG] is literally

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:55.800 align:middle line:84%
something like "the
(____) you bought."

00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:57.570 align:middle line:84%
So that [TAGALOG]
is the kind of thing

00:11:57.570 --> 00:12:01.000 align:middle line:84%
that only ever goes
before noun phrases.

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:05.310 align:middle line:84%
So this is the Tagalog for "What
was the thing that you bought?"

00:12:05.310 --> 00:12:06.570 align:middle line:90%
or "the one that you bought."

00:12:06.570 --> 00:12:10.260 align:middle line:84%
And Tagalog, the word for "one,"
the word for "thing" is null.

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:11.310 align:middle line:90%
It's not pronounced.

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:12.570 align:middle line:90%
I think I got asked last time.

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:14.940 align:middle line:84%
Are there ever things in
trees that are not pronounced.

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:16.390 align:middle line:90%
And here's an example.

00:12:16.390 --> 00:12:18.303 align:middle line:84%
The Tagalog word for
"one" is such a thing.

00:12:18.303 --> 00:12:19.095 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Question.

00:12:19.095 --> 00:12:21.358 align:middle line:84%
So there are [? a ?]
type of sentences

00:12:21.358 --> 00:12:23.400 align:middle line:84%
which is more like, if
you didn't hear something,

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:24.160 align:middle line:90%
you want to clarify.

00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:26.310 align:middle line:84%
For example, you want to
say the question of, what?

00:12:26.310 --> 00:12:27.210 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes.

00:12:27.210 --> 00:12:27.710 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:12:27.710 --> 00:12:31.210 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And I see that there's
slightly different structure.

00:12:31.210 --> 00:12:34.000 align:middle line:84%
It's very [INAUDIBLE] to say,
[INAUDIBLE] or something.

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:35.250 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:38.685 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But how does that
fit with the fact that wh

00:12:38.685 --> 00:12:39.600 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:41.225 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Is
never to the right?

00:12:41.225 --> 00:12:43.900 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so that's a
really nice example.

00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:46.380 align:middle line:84%
The fact about
questions like that

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:49.950 align:middle line:84%
is that the wh-word is sitting
where it would normally be.

00:12:49.950 --> 00:12:52.233 align:middle line:84%
It's not moving
at all, because it

00:12:52.233 --> 00:12:54.150 align:middle line:84%
doesn't have to be at
the end of the sentence.

00:12:54.150 --> 00:13:01.560 align:middle line:84%
If I say, "Mary bought an
Estonian novel in Paris,"

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:05.850 align:middle line:84%
your response to that could be,
"Mary bought what in Paris?"

00:13:05.850 --> 00:13:07.290 align:middle line:84%
So the word for
"what" isn't going

00:13:07.290 --> 00:13:08.770 align:middle line:90%
at the end of the sentence.

00:13:08.770 --> 00:13:09.960 align:middle line:90%
In fact, it can't.

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:13.050 align:middle line:84%
It's not the natural way
to-- that's the natural way

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:13.920 align:middle line:90%
to respond to that.

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:17.400 align:middle line:84%
You don't say, "Mary
bought in Paris what?"

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:19.470 align:middle line:84%
So the word "what" isn't
going at the end of--

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:22.080 align:middle line:84%
it happens to be at the end of
the sentence in your example,

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:25.000 align:middle line:84%
but what it's really
doing is just not moving.

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:26.280 align:middle line:90%
It's just staying where it is.

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:27.655 align:middle line:84%
That's a really
nice point, which

00:13:27.655 --> 00:13:30.300 align:middle line:84%
I was planning to make later,
but thank you for making it

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:32.970 align:middle line:84%
now, that we have to draw
this distinction between just

00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:35.400 align:middle line:84%
sitting there and
moving to the right.

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:37.200 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so in those
kinds of questions,

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:38.408 align:middle line:90%
it's not moving to the right.

00:13:38.408 --> 00:13:39.360 align:middle line:90%
Nice, nice example.

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:40.710 align:middle line:90%
Does that answer your question?

00:13:40.710 --> 00:13:41.580 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:44.100 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, no questions?

00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:45.810 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:13:45.810 --> 00:13:48.390 align:middle line:84%
OK, so this is the
setup for this point.

00:13:48.390 --> 00:13:50.970 align:middle line:84%
There are no languages that
move wh-words to the right,

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:54.630 align:middle line:84%
but I'm going to show
you a weird wiggle that

00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:56.260 align:middle line:90%
obscures that fact sometimes.

00:13:56.260 --> 00:13:58.980 align:middle line:84%
So first, there are
languages like Tagalog

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:01.608 align:middle line:84%
in which you can't ask,
"What did you buy?"

00:14:01.608 --> 00:14:03.150 align:middle line:84%
You have to say
something like, "What

00:14:03.150 --> 00:14:05.250 align:middle line:90%
was the thing that you bought?"

00:14:05.250 --> 00:14:07.440 align:middle line:84%
And I just have to
ask you to trust me

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:10.650 align:middle line:84%
that that's the best analysis
of this Tagalog question.

00:14:10.650 --> 00:14:11.430 align:middle line:90%
I work on Tagalog.

00:14:11.430 --> 00:14:13.715 align:middle line:84%
I'm happy to talk about
this at great length.

00:14:13.715 --> 00:14:16.090 align:middle line:84%
Talking about Tagalog is one
of my favorite things to do.

00:14:16.090 --> 00:14:18.763 align:middle line:84%
So don't ask me any questions
about this Tagalog question.

00:14:18.763 --> 00:14:19.680 align:middle line:90%
That's very dangerous.

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:22.050 align:middle line:84%
If you want to learn
anything else today,

00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:25.560 align:middle line:84%
we have to get away
from this slide, OK?

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:31.750 align:middle line:84%
All right, now, imagine-- so
Tagalog is a language that

00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:32.710 align:middle line:90%
has these clefts.

00:14:32.710 --> 00:14:36.790 align:middle line:84%
It has these bi-clausal ways of
asking questions, wh-questions.

00:14:36.790 --> 00:14:38.950 align:middle line:84%
And it has obligatory
wh-movement.

00:14:38.950 --> 00:14:42.320 align:middle line:84%
So the Tagalog wh-words, they're
just like the English wh-words.

00:14:42.320 --> 00:14:45.910 align:middle line:84%
They have to go at the
beginning of the sentence.

00:14:45.910 --> 00:14:50.080 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, now imagine what a
language would be like, though,

00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:53.380 align:middle line:84%
if it had clefting
and it had wh-in-situ.

00:14:53.380 --> 00:14:58.130 align:middle line:84%
Well, then, it would
have questions like this.

00:14:58.130 --> 00:15:01.120 align:middle line:84%
It would say things like, "The
one that you bought was what?"

00:15:01.120 --> 00:15:03.820 align:middle line:84%
Or, "The one that
ate the meat was who?

00:15:03.820 --> 00:15:07.520 align:middle line:84%
That is, you'd have
that embedded clause,

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:10.630 align:middle line:84%
the embedded clause of the
cleft, and then the wh-word--

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:12.940 align:middle line:84%
which is kind of the
predicate, the thing

00:15:12.940 --> 00:15:14.980 align:middle line:84%
that's being
described as the thing

00:15:14.980 --> 00:15:17.530 align:middle line:84%
that this embedded
clause applies to--

00:15:17.530 --> 00:15:19.342 align:middle line:84%
would end up at the
end of the sentence--

00:15:19.342 --> 00:15:21.550 align:middle line:84%
or at least it could, if
that's where predicates went

00:15:21.550 --> 00:15:22.758 align:middle line:90%
in the language in question--

00:15:22.758 --> 00:15:26.350 align:middle line:90%


00:15:26.350 --> 00:15:28.270 align:middle line:84%
but not because
the wh moved there,

00:15:28.270 --> 00:15:29.500 align:middle line:90%
just because it was in situ.

00:15:29.500 --> 00:15:32.980 align:middle line:84%
It would be like Vlada's
example of a second ago, right?

00:15:32.980 --> 00:15:36.490 align:middle line:84%
So Vlada was saying,
here's an example

00:15:36.490 --> 00:15:38.740 align:middle line:84%
where the wh-word is at
the end of a sentence.

00:15:38.740 --> 00:15:41.080 align:middle line:84%
And I was saying, yes,
but it's by accident.

00:15:41.080 --> 00:15:42.820 align:middle line:84%
It's really just
staying in situ.

00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:45.050 align:middle line:84%
It's not moving to the
end of the sentence.

00:15:45.050 --> 00:15:47.412 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:15:47.412 --> 00:15:49.870 align:middle line:84%
I'm saying all this because
there are languages like this--

00:15:49.870 --> 00:15:53.050 align:middle line:90%


00:15:53.050 --> 00:15:55.870 align:middle line:84%
languages where the wh-word
is at the end of the sentence,

00:15:55.870 --> 00:15:57.880 align:middle line:84%
not because it's
move to the right,

00:15:57.880 --> 00:16:02.922 align:middle line:84%
but because, well, the language
has clefts, and the wh-word

00:16:02.922 --> 00:16:04.630 align:middle line:84%
always ends up at the
end of the sentence

00:16:04.630 --> 00:16:07.510 align:middle line:84%
not because it moves there,
but because it's in situ.

00:16:07.510 --> 00:16:09.530 align:middle line:84%
Kabardian is a
language like this.

00:16:09.530 --> 00:16:12.100 align:middle line:84%
And if you thought I was going
to pronounce this Kabardian

00:16:12.100 --> 00:16:14.200 align:middle line:84%
example for you, you have
another think coming.

00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:16.643 align:middle line:84%
I have no hope of pronouncing
this Kabardian example.

00:16:16.643 --> 00:16:19.060 align:middle line:84%
But this is how you say "Who
ate the meat?" in Kabardian--

00:16:19.060 --> 00:16:22.075 align:middle line:84%
the only thing I know how to
say in Kabardian where I don't

00:16:22.075 --> 00:16:23.200 align:middle line:90%
in fact know how to say it.

00:16:23.200 --> 00:16:25.150 align:middle line:90%
I only know what it looks like.

00:16:25.150 --> 00:16:27.790 align:middle line:90%
I can't pronounce it.

00:16:27.790 --> 00:16:31.810 align:middle line:84%
And it is literally, "The
one who ate the meat is who?"

00:16:31.810 --> 00:16:34.480 align:middle line:84%
Like that's how you
say that in Kabardian.

00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:37.240 align:middle line:84%
So there actually are languages
that put their wh-words

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:38.350 align:middle line:90%
at the ends of sentences.

00:16:38.350 --> 00:16:39.460 align:middle line:90%
Kabardian is one.

00:16:39.460 --> 00:16:43.270 align:middle line:84%
But the structure never involves
wh-movement to the right.

00:16:43.270 --> 00:16:46.840 align:middle line:84%
It's always clefting
with the wh-in-situ.

00:16:46.840 --> 00:16:49.870 align:middle line:84%
The conversation with the
person in the airplane

00:16:49.870 --> 00:16:51.170 align:middle line:90%
is always over by this point.

00:16:51.170 --> 00:16:53.795 align:middle line:84%
But if they would listen to me,
this is what I would tell them.

00:16:53.795 --> 00:16:59.660 align:middle line:84%
It's yeah, you don't want a
story that says, wh-words--

00:16:59.660 --> 00:17:01.690 align:middle line:84%
it's natural for wh-words
to be at the beginning

00:17:01.690 --> 00:17:02.357 align:middle line:90%
of the sentence.

00:17:02.357 --> 00:17:03.968 align:middle line:84%
First of all, you
then have to tell me

00:17:03.968 --> 00:17:05.260 align:middle line:90%
what the heck you mean by that.

00:17:05.260 --> 00:17:09.442 align:middle line:84%
But there actually are languages
in which the wh-word always

00:17:09.442 --> 00:17:10.900 align:middle line:84%
ends up at the end
of the sentence.

00:17:10.900 --> 00:17:12.654 align:middle line:84%
It's just that it
doesn't move there.

00:17:12.654 --> 00:17:14.154 align:middle line:84%
It's always in situ
when it's there.

00:17:14.154 --> 00:17:15.175 align:middle line:90%
Faith?

00:17:15.175 --> 00:17:17.170 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So I'm thinking
about in Spanish,

00:17:17.170 --> 00:17:20.243 align:middle line:84%
how a direct object pronoun
can go before or after a verb.

00:17:20.243 --> 00:17:21.160 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:17:21.160 --> 00:17:25.215 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But there's always,
like, leftward wh-movement,

00:17:25.215 --> 00:17:27.990 align:middle line:84%
because you could say,
look, "Lo quiero decir"

00:17:27.990 --> 00:17:30.240 align:middle line:84%
or "Quiero decirlo," but
it's always going to be "¿Qué

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:30.865 align:middle line:90%
quieres decir?"

00:17:30.865 --> 00:17:32.876 align:middle line:90%
Is there a reason for that?

00:17:32.876 --> 00:17:37.330 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So
Spanish is a language

00:17:37.330 --> 00:17:39.280 align:middle line:84%
that has been described
as having wh-movement.

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:41.980 align:middle line:84%
Usually, there's
supposed to be contexts

00:17:41.980 --> 00:17:45.520 align:middle line:84%
in which it's OK to say the
Spanish equivalent of "and you

00:17:45.520 --> 00:17:47.005 align:middle line:90%
bought what?"

00:17:47.005 --> 00:17:49.750 align:middle line:84%
where the wh-word ends up
at the end of the sentence.

00:17:49.750 --> 00:17:53.110 align:middle line:84%
And those are supposed to not be
exactly like the English cases

00:17:53.110 --> 00:17:56.230 align:middle line:90%
of being astonished.

00:17:56.230 --> 00:17:58.540 align:middle line:84%
What you really wanted to
know about was the pronoun--

00:17:58.540 --> 00:18:01.450 align:middle line:84%
like why can you
say, in Spanish,

00:18:01.450 --> 00:18:07.060 align:middle line:84%
either "He wants to say it,"
or "It he wants to say," Right?

00:18:07.060 --> 00:18:09.910 align:middle line:84%
So Spanish has these
words-- they're called

00:18:09.910 --> 00:18:11.440 align:middle line:90%
"clitics" in the literature.

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:14.860 align:middle line:84%
It's common for pronouns to be
like this in a lot of languages

00:18:14.860 --> 00:18:17.830 align:middle line:84%
where they have special
rules about where

00:18:17.830 --> 00:18:19.510 align:middle line:90%
they go in the sentence.

00:18:19.510 --> 00:18:22.840 align:middle line:84%
I have only shown you one
kind of movement, wh-movement,

00:18:22.840 --> 00:18:25.900 align:middle line:84%
but what you're pointing out
is that there are other kinds.

00:18:25.900 --> 00:18:28.750 align:middle line:84%
So Spanish is a language that
more or less has to move its

00:18:28.750 --> 00:18:32.050 align:middle line:84%
wh-phrases, but its clitics
have special conditions on where

00:18:32.050 --> 00:18:35.590 align:middle line:84%
they go that are
distinct from its--

00:18:35.590 --> 00:18:36.190 align:middle line:90%
from a--

00:18:36.190 --> 00:18:39.670 align:middle line:84%
It's important for your example
that it involves an infinitive,

00:18:39.670 --> 00:18:40.360 align:middle line:90%
I think--

00:18:40.360 --> 00:18:45.910 align:middle line:84%
that if you wanted to say,
whatever, "he said it,"

00:18:45.910 --> 00:18:47.260 align:middle line:90%
that you would say--

00:18:47.260 --> 00:18:48.850 align:middle line:90%
what would you say-- "it"?--

00:18:48.850 --> 00:18:49.810 align:middle line:90%
"Lo dijo." yeah.

00:18:49.810 --> 00:18:52.270 align:middle line:90%
So you wouldn't say "Dijo lo."

00:18:52.270 --> 00:18:56.770 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I think that
is a thing in Castilian Spanish

00:18:56.770 --> 00:19:00.150 align:middle line:84%
from the 1600s, is
the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:19:00.150 --> 00:19:01.900 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: It's
a point of variation

00:19:01.900 --> 00:19:03.200 align:middle line:90%
between Romance languages.

00:19:03.200 --> 00:19:06.175 align:middle line:84%
So there are Romance languages--
and languages, generally.

00:19:06.175 --> 00:19:09.340 align:middle line:84%
There are Romance--
so these are languages

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:11.937 align:middle line:84%
in which the clitic wants
to attach to the verb,

00:19:11.937 --> 00:19:14.020 align:middle line:84%
and whether it goes before
or after the verb often

00:19:14.020 --> 00:19:17.390 align:middle line:84%
depends on whether the verb
is an infinitival verb or not.

00:19:17.390 --> 00:19:19.660 align:middle line:84%
And for Spanish,
I think, the rules

00:19:19.660 --> 00:19:23.800 align:middle line:84%
are it goes before tensed verbs
and after infinitival verbs.

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:27.580 align:middle line:84%
So your first example had both
an infinitive and a tensed verb

00:19:27.580 --> 00:19:29.650 align:middle line:84%
in it, and the clitic
had two options

00:19:29.650 --> 00:19:31.482 align:middle line:90%
as to where it could go.

00:19:31.482 --> 00:19:33.940 align:middle line:84%
If we were developing a complete
theory of clitics-- which,

00:19:33.940 --> 00:19:36.047 align:middle line:90%
just to be clear, we are not--

00:19:36.047 --> 00:19:38.380 align:middle line:84%
we'd want to have an account
of that, figuring that out.

00:19:38.380 --> 00:19:40.660 align:middle line:84%
The Phenomenon is called
"clitic climbing."

00:19:40.660 --> 00:19:42.640 align:middle line:84%
The clitic that belongs
to the embedded verb

00:19:42.640 --> 00:19:45.382 align:middle line:84%
gets to climb up and
attach to the first verb.

00:19:45.382 --> 00:19:47.470 align:middle line:90%
Was there another hand?

00:19:47.470 --> 00:19:50.070 align:middle line:84%
I thought all of you
had hands, but I guess

00:19:50.070 --> 00:19:51.940 align:middle line:90%
they're all sitting peacefully.

00:19:51.940 --> 00:19:55.860 align:middle line:90%
OK, cool.

00:19:55.860 --> 00:19:59.220 align:middle line:84%
OK, so language universal,
there is no true wh-movement

00:19:59.220 --> 00:19:59.910 align:middle line:90%
to the right.

00:19:59.910 --> 00:20:02.100 align:middle line:84%
There is Kabardian,
so wh-in-situ

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:03.690 align:middle line:84%
combined with the
obligatory clefting,

00:20:03.690 --> 00:20:06.240 align:middle line:84%
with the consequence that the wh
is at the end of the sentence.

00:20:06.240 --> 00:20:08.130 align:middle line:84%
But there's never a
wh-movement to the right.

00:20:08.130 --> 00:20:10.110 align:middle line:84%
If you're a
syntactician, you look

00:20:10.110 --> 00:20:13.140 align:middle line:84%
at this fact about the world,
and you think, well, why?

00:20:13.140 --> 00:20:17.490 align:middle line:84%
Why is wh-movement, why is
the specifier of CP the--

00:20:17.490 --> 00:20:22.860 align:middle line:84%
we go back to the tree
over here, this tree--

00:20:22.860 --> 00:20:26.010 align:middle line:84%
that wh-movement that lands
in the specifier of CP

00:20:26.010 --> 00:20:28.770 align:middle line:84%
that makes the noun phrase,
what, a daughter of CP?

00:20:28.770 --> 00:20:31.770 align:middle line:84%
Why does that daughter of
CP always precede C-bar?

00:20:31.770 --> 00:20:33.000 align:middle line:90%
Why can't it follow C-bar?

00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:35.542 align:middle line:84%
There's no language anywhere on
Earth where it follows C-bar,

00:20:35.542 --> 00:20:36.990 align:middle line:84%
and it would be
nice to know why.

00:20:36.990 --> 00:20:39.450 align:middle line:84%
So there's syntactic
work on why, which

00:20:39.450 --> 00:20:40.590 align:middle line:90%
I will not try to show you.

00:20:40.590 --> 00:20:43.200 align:middle line:84%
If you would like to know more,
take more linguistics classes.

00:20:43.200 --> 00:20:47.150 align:middle line:90%


00:20:47.150 --> 00:20:49.020 align:middle line:90%
OK, all right.

00:20:49.020 --> 00:20:52.235 align:middle line:84%
And then, yep--
and then, and this

00:20:52.235 --> 00:20:54.360 align:middle line:84%
is the part that I went
through too fast last time.

00:20:54.360 --> 00:20:55.850 align:middle line:84%
And so I'm going to go through
it a little more slowly

00:20:55.850 --> 00:20:56.270 align:middle line:90%
this time.

00:20:56.270 --> 00:20:56.640 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:20:56.640 --> 00:20:57.140 align:middle line:90%
We'll see.

00:20:57.140 --> 00:21:00.520 align:middle line:90%


00:21:00.520 --> 00:21:04.840 align:middle line:84%
That kind of generalization
about languages

00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:09.640 align:middle line:84%
not being completely
varied, it's not the case.

00:21:09.640 --> 00:21:15.695 align:middle line:84%
There-- there's a phenomenon,
wh-movement, and there are--

00:21:15.695 --> 00:21:17.320 align:middle line:84%
there's more than
one kind of language.

00:21:17.320 --> 00:21:19.923 align:middle line:84%
There are languages that do
wh-movement like English,

00:21:19.923 --> 00:21:21.340 align:middle line:84%
and there are
languages that leave

00:21:21.340 --> 00:21:26.930 align:middle line:84%
the wh-in-situ, like Mandarin
or Kabardian, but that's it.

00:21:26.930 --> 00:21:28.840 align:middle line:84%
Those are all the
kinds that there are.

00:21:28.840 --> 00:21:30.930 align:middle line:84%
And there are imaginable
kinds, like the mirror

00:21:30.930 --> 00:21:32.680 align:middle line:84%
image of English, where
the wh-phrase goes

00:21:32.680 --> 00:21:34.540 align:middle line:90%
at the end, which don't exist.

00:21:34.540 --> 00:21:36.033 align:middle line:90%
And we'd like to know why.

00:21:36.033 --> 00:21:38.200 align:middle line:84%
I want to show you another
example of the same kind.

00:21:38.200 --> 00:21:41.260 align:middle line:84%
I showed you this last time,
but I'll show it to you again.

00:21:41.260 --> 00:21:42.880 align:middle line:84%
There's also
variation with respect

00:21:42.880 --> 00:21:45.110 align:middle line:84%
to how you do
multiple wh-questions,

00:21:45.110 --> 00:21:47.050 align:middle line:84%
So wh-questions where
there's more than one

00:21:47.050 --> 00:21:49.762 align:middle line:90%
wh-word in the sentence.

00:21:49.762 --> 00:21:51.970 align:middle line:84%
First of all, there are
languages that just ban them.

00:21:51.970 --> 00:21:53.480 align:middle line:90%
That's one kind of language.

00:21:53.480 --> 00:21:56.140 align:middle line:90%
So Irish doesn't allow them.

00:21:56.140 --> 00:21:58.330 align:middle line:90%
Italian, at least until--

00:21:58.330 --> 00:22:00.070 align:middle line:84%
apparently, young
Italians are beginning

00:22:00.070 --> 00:22:01.990 align:middle line:84%
to develop
multiple-wh questions.

00:22:01.990 --> 00:22:03.370 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:03.370 --> 00:22:06.340 align:middle line:84%
But older Italians
don't have them.

00:22:06.340 --> 00:22:09.760 align:middle line:84%
Kids today and their
multiple-wh questions!

00:22:09.760 --> 00:22:12.970 align:middle line:84%
And then, so for languages
that do have them,

00:22:12.970 --> 00:22:16.720 align:middle line:84%
they're languages like
English in which the rule is,

00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:19.420 align:middle line:84%
English has
wh-movement, as we know,

00:22:19.420 --> 00:22:23.222 align:middle line:84%
and if you have more than one
wh-word, the rule for English

00:22:23.222 --> 00:22:24.430 align:middle line:90%
is that you move one of them.

00:22:24.430 --> 00:22:28.030 align:middle line:84%
So you ask questions like,
"What did you give to whom?"

00:22:28.030 --> 00:22:30.610 align:middle line:84%
Or "What did you
give to whom when?"

00:22:30.610 --> 00:22:32.288 align:middle line:84%
If you have three
wh-phrases, you're

00:22:32.288 --> 00:22:33.580 align:middle line:90%
only going to move one of them.

00:22:33.580 --> 00:22:36.660 align:middle line:84%
That's the generalization
about English.

00:22:36.660 --> 00:22:38.700 align:middle line:84%
And then-- I told
you this last time--

00:22:38.700 --> 00:22:41.310 align:middle line:84%
there are languages like
Bulgarian and Mohawk

00:22:41.310 --> 00:22:43.470 align:middle line:84%
in which all of
the wh-words move.

00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:47.040 align:middle line:84%
So in Bulgarian, you must say,
"What to whom did he give?"

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:48.420 align:middle line:90%
That's that second question.

00:22:48.420 --> 00:22:50.040 align:middle line:84%
Or in Mohawk, you
say things like,

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:52.200 align:middle line:90%
"Tell me who what bought."

00:22:52.200 --> 00:22:55.005 align:middle line:84%
You can't leave either of
the wh-phrases in situ.

00:22:55.005 --> 00:22:58.210 align:middle line:90%


00:22:58.210 --> 00:23:00.850 align:middle line:84%
And so here's the place
where I promised you,

00:23:00.850 --> 00:23:04.010 align:middle line:84%
there's more than
one kind of language,

00:23:04.010 --> 00:23:06.352 align:middle line:84%
but there isn't every
imaginable kind of language.

00:23:06.352 --> 00:23:07.810 align:middle line:84%
So there are
languages like English

00:23:07.810 --> 00:23:09.040 align:middle line:90%
that move one wh-phrase.

00:23:09.040 --> 00:23:11.290 align:middle line:84%
There are languages like
Mandarin that don't move any.

00:23:11.290 --> 00:23:13.780 align:middle line:84%
And there are languages
like Bulgarian and Mohawk

00:23:13.780 --> 00:23:15.040 align:middle line:90%
that move all of them.

00:23:15.040 --> 00:23:20.260 align:middle line:84%
But 0 and 1 and "all"
are all of the options

00:23:20.260 --> 00:23:23.470 align:middle line:84%
for moving wh-phrases in
multiple wh-questions.

00:23:23.470 --> 00:23:27.070 align:middle line:84%
And you all know
more math than I do.

00:23:27.070 --> 00:23:29.440 align:middle line:84%
You know of numbers
besides 0 and 1

00:23:29.440 --> 00:23:32.800 align:middle line:84%
and, well, "all," which
I guess isn't a number.

00:23:32.800 --> 00:23:34.630 align:middle line:84%
So you could imagine
a language where

00:23:34.630 --> 00:23:38.230 align:middle line:84%
the rule is, move any
number of wh-phrases

00:23:38.230 --> 00:23:40.300 align:middle line:90%
up to a maximum of 2, right?

00:23:40.300 --> 00:23:43.210 align:middle line:84%
Or move any number of
wh-phrases, I don't know,

00:23:43.210 --> 00:23:46.960 align:middle line:84%
as long as it's
prime, or whatever--

00:23:46.960 --> 00:23:50.080 align:middle line:84%
as long as it's one of
the first 10 digits of pi.

00:23:50.080 --> 00:23:52.810 align:middle line:84%
All kinds of
imaginable languages

00:23:52.810 --> 00:23:56.290 align:middle line:84%
that you could imagine, and
none of them exist, right?

00:23:56.290 --> 00:23:58.090 align:middle line:84%
So there aren't
languages out there

00:23:58.090 --> 00:24:01.330 align:middle line:84%
in which you say "Who
gave a book to Mary?"

00:24:01.330 --> 00:24:03.760 align:middle line:84%
and you say "Who
what gave to Mary?"

00:24:03.760 --> 00:24:06.310 align:middle line:84%
but when you get
to three, you have

00:24:06.310 --> 00:24:08.050 align:middle line:90%
to say "Who what gave to whom?"

00:24:08.050 --> 00:24:09.310 align:middle line:90%
That language doesn't exist.

00:24:09.310 --> 00:24:10.360 align:middle line:90%
It's not hard to imagine.

00:24:10.360 --> 00:24:11.735 align:middle line:84%
You could write
a grammar for it.

00:24:11.735 --> 00:24:12.760 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't exist.

00:24:12.760 --> 00:24:15.980 align:middle line:90%
You don't find it.

00:24:15.980 --> 00:24:18.370 align:middle line:84%
And then, this is
where I gave you

00:24:18.370 --> 00:24:21.010 align:middle line:84%
the parable of the
function, which

00:24:21.010 --> 00:24:24.640 align:middle line:84%
was meant to be a
parable about what life

00:24:24.640 --> 00:24:26.800 align:middle line:90%
is like when you are a baby.

00:24:26.800 --> 00:24:30.790 align:middle line:84%
So imagine, I said, that
you are a Bulgarian baby,

00:24:30.790 --> 00:24:35.410 align:middle line:84%
and you're hearing your parents
utter multiple wh-questions.

00:24:35.410 --> 00:24:39.370 align:middle line:84%
There's some limit on the
amount of data that you get--

00:24:39.370 --> 00:24:41.710 align:middle line:84%
not just if you're
a Bulgarian baby

00:24:41.710 --> 00:24:43.210 align:middle line:84%
listening to multiple
wh-questions,

00:24:43.210 --> 00:24:45.190 align:middle line:90%
but in life, right?

00:24:45.190 --> 00:24:47.660 align:middle line:84%
There is some-- it may not have
seemed like it at the time,

00:24:47.660 --> 00:24:50.350 align:middle line:84%
but there is some maximum amount
of stuff that your parents said

00:24:50.350 --> 00:24:54.048 align:middle line:84%
to you when you were growing up,
and the stuff that you heard--

00:24:54.048 --> 00:24:56.590 align:middle line:84%
not just your parents, but the
other people that were talking

00:24:56.590 --> 00:24:58.120 align:middle line:84%
to you when you were
growing up and acquiring

00:24:58.120 --> 00:24:59.080 align:middle line:90%
your native language.

00:24:59.080 --> 00:25:01.810 align:middle line:84%
There is some body of data--
maybe it was fairly large--

00:25:01.810 --> 00:25:03.880 align:middle line:84%
of things that
people said to you.

00:25:03.880 --> 00:25:06.280 align:middle line:84%
Maybe fairly large,
but it was finite,

00:25:06.280 --> 00:25:10.900 align:middle line:84%
so there was some maximum number
of wh-words in the questions

00:25:10.900 --> 00:25:13.180 align:middle line:90%
that you heard growing up.

00:25:13.180 --> 00:25:15.910 align:middle line:84%
And the thing
about finite data--

00:25:15.910 --> 00:25:18.640 align:middle line:84%
so the point of the parable
of the function-- is here,

00:25:18.640 --> 00:25:19.990 align:middle line:90%
I am giving you finite data.

00:25:19.990 --> 00:25:22.930 align:middle line:84%
The function returns 1 for
1, and 2 for 2, and 3 for 3,

00:25:22.930 --> 00:25:25.630 align:middle line:84%
and 4 for 4, and you
have no idea at all

00:25:25.630 --> 00:25:28.510 align:middle line:90%
what it should return for 5.

00:25:28.510 --> 00:25:31.150 align:middle line:84%
Given what I've
given you so far,

00:25:31.150 --> 00:25:34.030 align:middle line:84%
you might hope that
you're living in just

00:25:34.030 --> 00:25:36.970 align:middle line:84%
and merciful universe
where people, data work

00:25:36.970 --> 00:25:38.000 align:middle line:90%
the way they should.

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:42.460 align:middle line:84%
But they're just-- as a matter
of logic or a matter of math,

00:25:42.460 --> 00:25:45.150 align:middle line:84%
there are no restrictions
at all on what

00:25:45.150 --> 00:25:49.420 align:middle line:84%
the consequence of giving
this function 5 could be.

00:25:49.420 --> 00:25:51.790 align:middle line:84%
So I showed you one
example, one function

00:25:51.790 --> 00:25:54.400 align:middle line:84%
for which the
output would be 29,

00:25:54.400 --> 00:25:57.670 align:middle line:84%
and I could multiply
that first thing,

00:25:57.670 --> 00:25:59.860 align:middle line:84%
the thing that comes right
after the equals sign,

00:25:59.860 --> 00:26:02.260 align:middle line:84%
I could multiply that
by any number at all.

00:26:02.260 --> 00:26:05.860 align:middle line:84%
The consequence of f of
5 could just be anything.

00:26:05.860 --> 00:26:07.690 align:middle line:90%
And life is kind of like that.

00:26:07.690 --> 00:26:10.660 align:middle line:84%
This is sometimes called
the problem of induction

00:26:10.660 --> 00:26:13.600 align:middle line:84%
when people talk about it
in the context of science.

00:26:13.600 --> 00:26:18.730 align:middle line:84%
So you're going to make
some number of observations

00:26:18.730 --> 00:26:22.390 align:middle line:84%
of the world, and you'll
maybe see the same thing

00:26:22.390 --> 00:26:23.290 align:middle line:90%
over and over again.

00:26:23.290 --> 00:26:28.750 align:middle line:84%
You'll see that, as
you drop a weight,

00:26:28.750 --> 00:26:31.750 align:middle line:84%
that the weight falls down,
and not sideways or up.

00:26:31.750 --> 00:26:33.790 align:middle line:84%
And you see that over,
and over, and over again.

00:26:33.790 --> 00:26:36.250 align:middle line:84%
And eventually, you
start to hypothesize

00:26:36.250 --> 00:26:39.850 align:middle line:84%
that down is the direction
that they're going to fall,

00:26:39.850 --> 00:26:43.180 align:middle line:84%
that every crow you see
is going to be black,

00:26:43.180 --> 00:26:45.070 align:middle line:84%
that every swan you see
is going to be white,

00:26:45.070 --> 00:26:47.500 align:middle line:84%
because you keep seeing that
over and over and over again.

00:26:47.500 --> 00:26:51.850 align:middle line:84%
But it's kind of like concluding
that f of 5 is going to be 5.

00:26:51.850 --> 00:26:54.910 align:middle line:84%
It should work that way given
everything that you've seen,

00:26:54.910 --> 00:26:56.200 align:middle line:90%
but it might not.

00:26:56.200 --> 00:27:00.280 align:middle line:84%
Maybe the next weight you
drop will fall upwards.

00:27:00.280 --> 00:27:03.100 align:middle line:84%
And so part of
science is saying,

00:27:03.100 --> 00:27:04.960 align:middle line:90%
we're going to draw these laws.

00:27:04.960 --> 00:27:08.680 align:middle line:84%
Given the inevitably
finite data that we've got,

00:27:08.680 --> 00:27:10.963 align:middle line:84%
we're going to sketch
these laws about what

00:27:10.963 --> 00:27:12.130 align:middle line:90%
we think is going to happen.

00:27:12.130 --> 00:27:16.840 align:middle line:84%
And then we go on doing
science and refining our laws.

00:27:16.840 --> 00:27:18.040 align:middle line:90%
Raquel, are you-- yeah?

00:27:18.040 --> 00:27:19.720 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The thought
that I am thinking

00:27:19.720 --> 00:27:21.730 align:middle line:84%
is something along
the lines of like,

00:27:21.730 --> 00:27:23.980 align:middle line:84%
is it possible that
part of being human

00:27:23.980 --> 00:27:28.450 align:middle line:84%
is developing languages that are
kinder than giving you "f of 5

00:27:28.450 --> 00:27:34.570 align:middle line:84%
is 29," and that maybe it's
like you could give languages

00:27:34.570 --> 00:27:39.550 align:middle line:84%
a weird difficulty coefficient,
and then the coefficient,

00:27:39.550 --> 00:27:43.100 align:middle line:84%
if it's really low, tells
you that it'll probably be

00:27:43.100 --> 00:27:44.350 align:middle line:90%
something intuitive like five.

00:27:44.350 --> 00:27:46.420 align:middle line:84%
If it's really
high, then you can't

00:27:46.420 --> 00:27:49.150 align:middle line:84%
rely on the language to be nice
to you when it could be 29?

00:27:49.150 --> 00:27:50.980 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, that's--
you're saying it very well.

00:27:50.980 --> 00:27:53.740 align:middle line:84%
Maybe another way to say it,
to repeat what you just said,

00:27:53.740 --> 00:27:58.900 align:middle line:84%
is yeah, human minds are set up
in such a way that f of 5 is 5.

00:27:58.900 --> 00:28:02.710 align:middle line:84%
Human minds are set up in such a
way that multiple wh-questions,

00:28:02.710 --> 00:28:04.930 align:middle line:90%
there are only a few options.

00:28:04.930 --> 00:28:07.840 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'm learning Italian
and multiple wh-questions

00:28:07.840 --> 00:28:08.710 align:middle line:90%
are just banned.

00:28:08.710 --> 00:28:12.130 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'm learning Mandarin and
wh-phrases all stay in situ.

00:28:12.130 --> 00:28:14.200 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'm learning English
and one of them moves.

00:28:14.200 --> 00:28:16.283 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'm learning Bulgarian
and all of them move.

00:28:16.283 --> 00:28:17.150 align:middle line:90%
And that's it.

00:28:17.150 --> 00:28:19.510 align:middle line:84%
Those are the only
options there are.

00:28:19.510 --> 00:28:21.533 align:middle line:84%
Which means if you're
a Bulgarian child,

00:28:21.533 --> 00:28:23.950 align:middle line:84%
and you're growing up, and
you're hearing your parents ask

00:28:23.950 --> 00:28:27.280 align:middle line:84%
multiple wh-questions,
there's some maximum number

00:28:27.280 --> 00:28:30.430 align:middle line:84%
of wh-words you ever heard them
say at any point in their life.

00:28:30.430 --> 00:28:34.930 align:middle line:84%
And that number-- suppose
that's the most inquisitive

00:28:34.930 --> 00:28:36.520 align:middle line:84%
your parents ever
got in your life,

00:28:36.520 --> 00:28:39.520 align:middle line:90%
It was a two-wh-word question.

00:28:39.520 --> 00:28:43.850 align:middle line:84%
I can say, incidentally,
multiple-wh questions--

00:28:43.850 --> 00:28:46.330 align:middle line:84%
I wrote my dissertation
about multiple-wh questions.

00:28:46.330 --> 00:28:49.570 align:middle line:84%
They're extremely interesting,
but they're not common.

00:28:49.570 --> 00:28:54.580 align:middle line:84%
If you spend some
time listening,

00:28:54.580 --> 00:28:56.980 align:middle line:84%
you'll spend a long
time before you hear

00:28:56.980 --> 00:28:58.720 align:middle line:90%
somebody say this in the wild.

00:28:58.720 --> 00:29:02.170 align:middle line:84%
It's not a common kind
of question to ask.

00:29:02.170 --> 00:29:04.090 align:middle line:84%
I happen to know, for
example, that there

00:29:04.090 --> 00:29:07.330 align:middle line:84%
are no multiple
wh-questions in the Bible.

00:29:07.330 --> 00:29:08.575 align:middle line:90%
So I have this project--

00:29:08.575 --> 00:29:09.622 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:29:09.622 --> 00:29:11.580 align:middle line:84%
--that involves going
through the Bible verses.

00:29:11.580 --> 00:29:13.375 align:middle line:84%
So there's a language
called Wampanoag,

00:29:13.375 --> 00:29:16.030 align:middle line:84%
which is a language
that's spoken around here

00:29:16.030 --> 00:29:16.870 align:middle line:90%
traditionally.

00:29:16.870 --> 00:29:19.330 align:middle line:84%
The Wampanoag are trying to
revive their language, and one

00:29:19.330 --> 00:29:22.240 align:middle line:84%
of the texts that they have is
a complete Bible translation.

00:29:22.240 --> 00:29:24.840 align:middle line:84%
It was the first Bible
published in this hemisphere.

00:29:24.840 --> 00:29:27.430 align:middle line:84%
It was a Wampanoag Bible
that was published here

00:29:27.430 --> 00:29:30.550 align:middle line:90%
in Boston in the 1600s--

00:29:30.550 --> 00:29:32.500 align:middle line:90%
1635.

00:29:32.500 --> 00:29:34.720 align:middle line:84%
And so I have been reading
the Bible very slowly

00:29:34.720 --> 00:29:37.300 align:middle line:84%
and carefully, trying to
put the grammar of Wampanoag

00:29:37.300 --> 00:29:39.020 align:middle line:84%
back together and
writing a dictionary.

00:29:39.020 --> 00:29:42.518 align:middle line:84%
And so I really wish that there
were multiple-wh questions

00:29:42.518 --> 00:29:43.060 align:middle line:90%
in the Bible.

00:29:43.060 --> 00:29:45.143 align:middle line:84%
It would answer certain
questions about Wampanoag.

00:29:45.143 --> 00:29:48.038 align:middle line:84%
I would really like Jesus
Christ to turn to the disciples

00:29:48.038 --> 00:29:49.330 align:middle line:90%
and ask them "Who bought what?"

00:29:49.330 --> 00:29:50.410 align:middle line:90%
It would be great.

00:29:50.410 --> 00:29:51.040 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:29:51.040 --> 00:29:52.540 align:middle line:90%
But he never does, never.

00:29:52.540 --> 00:29:53.990 align:middle line:90%
I have checked.

00:29:53.990 --> 00:29:55.990 align:middle line:84%
There are no multiple-wh
questions in the Bible.

00:29:55.990 --> 00:29:57.550 align:middle line:84%
And the Bible is
quite large, right?

00:29:57.550 --> 00:29:58.902 align:middle line:90%
There's lots and lots of texts.

00:29:58.902 --> 00:30:00.610 align:middle line:84%
I know this because
I've been reading it.

00:30:00.610 --> 00:30:01.443 align:middle line:90%
It's taken me years.

00:30:01.443 --> 00:30:04.330 align:middle line:90%


00:30:04.330 --> 00:30:05.770 align:middle line:90%
So these are not common.

00:30:05.770 --> 00:30:08.590 align:middle line:84%
It's not at all-- it
wouldn't be at all surprising

00:30:08.590 --> 00:30:11.410 align:middle line:84%
if the largest multiple-wh
question you ever

00:30:11.410 --> 00:30:13.605 align:middle line:84%
heard your parents say
had two wh-phrases in it--

00:30:13.605 --> 00:30:14.980 align:middle line:84%
something like
"Who bought what?"

00:30:14.980 --> 00:30:16.960 align:middle line:90%
or "What did he give to whom?"

00:30:16.960 --> 00:30:18.910 align:middle line:84%
Maybe you heard your
parents at some point

00:30:18.910 --> 00:30:22.630 align:middle line:84%
say, "Who gave what
to whom, when, why?"

00:30:22.630 --> 00:30:24.010 align:middle line:90%
It's not likely.

00:30:24.010 --> 00:30:26.620 align:middle line:84%
And so I think I
said this last time.

00:30:26.620 --> 00:30:29.440 align:middle line:84%
You might have
expected if children

00:30:29.440 --> 00:30:32.200 align:middle line:84%
had to consider all of the
imaginable grammars that

00:30:32.200 --> 00:30:34.390 align:middle line:84%
are consistent with the
data that they have,

00:30:34.390 --> 00:30:38.620 align:middle line:84%
you might have imagined that
Bulgarian children would have--

00:30:38.620 --> 00:30:41.163 align:middle line:84%
Bulgarian adults would
have different grammars,

00:30:41.163 --> 00:30:42.580 align:middle line:84%
that there would
be Bulgarians who

00:30:42.580 --> 00:30:46.330 align:middle line:84%
guessed that the grammar
was move your wh-phrases up

00:30:46.330 --> 00:30:48.668 align:middle line:84%
to a maximum of two, or
move your wh-phrases up

00:30:48.668 --> 00:30:50.710 align:middle line:84%
to a maximum of three, or
move your wh-phrases up

00:30:50.710 --> 00:30:51.585 align:middle line:90%
to a maximum of four.

00:30:51.585 --> 00:30:52.990 align:middle line:90%
But that's not what we find.

00:30:52.990 --> 00:30:56.410 align:middle line:84%
So any Bulgarian that you
ask, as you ratchet up

00:30:56.410 --> 00:31:00.400 align:middle line:84%
the number of wh-words in the
question, they just always

00:31:00.400 --> 00:31:01.930 align:middle line:90%
move them all.

00:31:01.930 --> 00:31:03.940 align:middle line:84%
The only choice they
all made was all.

00:31:03.940 --> 00:31:10.450 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so here's a case where the
data that anybody is exposed to

00:31:10.450 --> 00:31:13.540 align:middle line:84%
are necessarily
limited in a way that

00:31:13.540 --> 00:31:16.660 align:middle line:84%
means that there are literally
infinitely many possible

00:31:16.660 --> 00:31:19.150 align:middle line:84%
grammars that are compatible
with the data that they have.

00:31:19.150 --> 00:31:22.300 align:middle line:84%
And yet, they all converge
on the same answer.

00:31:22.300 --> 00:31:26.170 align:middle line:84%
So this is a case of what
Raquel was just talking about.

00:31:26.170 --> 00:31:28.083 align:middle line:84%
It's as though
being a human being

00:31:28.083 --> 00:31:29.500 align:middle line:84%
means having the
kind of mind that

00:31:29.500 --> 00:31:31.940 align:middle line:84%
considers some possibilities
but not others.

00:31:31.940 --> 00:31:35.650 align:middle line:84%
So you consider all, but
you don't consider up

00:31:35.650 --> 00:31:36.850 align:middle line:90%
to a maximum of two.

00:31:36.850 --> 00:31:39.660 align:middle line:84%
That's not a kind of
grammar you think about.

00:31:39.660 --> 00:31:42.040 align:middle line:84%
That's apparently
what we're finding.

00:31:42.040 --> 00:31:43.870 align:middle line:84%
Raising questions
like, well, why?

00:31:43.870 --> 00:31:46.630 align:middle line:84%
So what is it about the human
mind that makes it like that?

00:31:46.630 --> 00:31:51.750 align:middle line:84%
That's one of the kinds of
things linguists think about.

00:31:51.750 --> 00:31:56.520 align:middle line:84%
OK, similarly-- and
this is echoing a point

00:31:56.520 --> 00:31:59.500 align:middle line:84%
that Vlada made a second ago--
if you're learning Chinese,

00:31:59.500 --> 00:32:01.675 align:middle line:84%
if you're growing
up learning Chinese,

00:32:01.675 --> 00:32:03.300 align:middle line:84%
you may hear your
parents say something

00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:05.740 align:middle line:90%
like this at some point.

00:32:05.740 --> 00:32:11.010 align:middle line:84%
And if you are considering
all of the logically possible

00:32:11.010 --> 00:32:12.930 align:middle line:84%
grammars that are
consistent with this,

00:32:12.930 --> 00:32:15.420 align:middle line:84%
you might have to spend
some time thinking, well,

00:32:15.420 --> 00:32:17.340 align:middle line:84%
am I learning a
language that moves

00:32:17.340 --> 00:32:19.860 align:middle line:84%
its wh-phrases to
the right, or am I

00:32:19.860 --> 00:32:23.160 align:middle line:84%
learning a language that
leaves its wh-phrases in situ?

00:32:23.160 --> 00:32:24.900 align:middle line:84%
And so you might
expect that there

00:32:24.900 --> 00:32:29.100 align:middle line:84%
would be a stage in the
life of Mandarin children

00:32:29.100 --> 00:32:32.250 align:middle line:84%
where they entertained the
possibility that wh-phrases

00:32:32.250 --> 00:32:33.100 align:middle line:90%
move to the right.

00:32:33.100 --> 00:32:36.180 align:middle line:84%
It would be a cute
kid thing to do.

00:32:36.180 --> 00:32:39.210 align:middle line:90%
Oh, our child is so cute.

00:32:39.210 --> 00:32:41.913 align:middle line:84%
When she was three, she thought
wh-phrases moved to the right.

00:32:41.913 --> 00:32:43.080 align:middle line:90%
She was saying things like--

00:32:43.080 --> 00:32:43.420 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Aww.

00:32:43.420 --> 00:32:44.545 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Aww, yeah.

00:32:44.545 --> 00:32:45.840 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:32:45.840 --> 00:32:48.690 align:middle line:84%
But no, I mean, Chinese
babies are cute like babies

00:32:48.690 --> 00:32:51.390 align:middle line:84%
everywhere, but they do not make
that particular cute mistake.

00:32:51.390 --> 00:32:53.280 align:middle line:84%
If you've ever been
around small children,

00:32:53.280 --> 00:32:55.440 align:middle line:84%
you know children
do make mistakes.

00:32:55.440 --> 00:32:59.070 align:middle line:84%
They take guesses that are
wrong about how the adult

00:32:59.070 --> 00:33:01.920 align:middle line:84%
language works, and
they spend some time

00:33:01.920 --> 00:33:03.240 align:middle line:90%
making adorable mistakes.

00:33:03.240 --> 00:33:05.580 align:middle line:84%
But the children do not make
that particular adorable

00:33:05.580 --> 00:33:08.910 align:middle line:84%
mistake because they
are human beings,

00:33:08.910 --> 00:33:11.370 align:middle line:84%
and they know that there
are on some level--

00:33:11.370 --> 00:33:14.340 align:middle line:84%
their heads are structured in
such a way that they cannot

00:33:14.340 --> 00:33:16.770 align:middle line:84%
entertain grammars where
wh-phrases move to the right.

00:33:16.770 --> 00:33:18.560 align:middle line:90%
There aren't any of those.

00:33:18.560 --> 00:33:20.310 align:middle line:84%
Part of our job as
linguists is to come up

00:33:20.310 --> 00:33:22.540 align:middle line:84%
with a theory that
explains that.

00:33:22.540 --> 00:33:25.380 align:middle line:84%
And when I say it's part
of our job as linguists,

00:33:25.380 --> 00:33:29.850 align:middle line:84%
I do not mean that I'm now
about to tell you the theory.

00:33:29.850 --> 00:33:32.250 align:middle line:84%
That's an active
topic of research,

00:33:32.250 --> 00:33:35.340 align:middle line:84%
people trying to figure out
why grammar works that way.

00:33:35.340 --> 00:33:38.220 align:middle line:84%
OK so yeah, if you
are a Chinese baby

00:33:38.220 --> 00:33:39.960 align:middle line:84%
and you hear that
sentence, you know

00:33:39.960 --> 00:33:42.420 align:middle line:84%
that you are hearing
a wh-in-situ language.

00:33:42.420 --> 00:33:44.520 align:middle line:84%
You don't have to
entertain possibilities

00:33:44.520 --> 00:33:47.370 align:middle line:84%
like a language in which the
wh-phrase moves to the right,

00:33:47.370 --> 00:33:49.230 align:middle line:84%
or, a language in
which the wh-phrase is

00:33:49.230 --> 00:33:51.610 align:middle line:90%
the third word in the sentence.

00:33:51.610 --> 00:33:55.500 align:middle line:84%
Those are both grammars that
would generate that question,

00:33:55.500 --> 00:33:57.330 align:middle line:84%
but you don't spend
any time entertaining

00:33:57.330 --> 00:33:59.100 align:middle line:90%
those possibilities.

00:33:59.100 --> 00:33:59.615 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:33:59.615 --> 00:34:00.990 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: OK,
well, this is going

00:34:00.990 --> 00:34:03.483 align:middle line:84%
to be probably a
bit like what if.

00:34:03.483 --> 00:34:04.400 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:34:04.400 --> 00:34:06.100 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Probably
never encounter it.

00:34:06.100 --> 00:34:08.239 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:34:08.239 --> 00:34:11.139 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Because you'd
think that nowhere

00:34:11.139 --> 00:34:17.380 align:middle line:84%
does [INAUDIBLE] Chinese
babies say, or like, think,

00:34:17.380 --> 00:34:19.715 align:middle line:90%
I must move word to end.

00:34:19.715 --> 00:34:21.219 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:34:21.219 --> 00:34:26.020 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What if,
hypothetically, a baby speaking

00:34:26.020 --> 00:34:27.820 align:middle line:90%
in an English--

00:34:27.820 --> 00:34:32.020 align:middle line:84%
a baby in an English-speaking
environment never heard

00:34:32.020 --> 00:34:34.320 align:middle line:90%
an in-situ--

00:34:34.320 --> 00:34:35.660 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: wh, mm-hmm.

00:34:35.660 --> 00:34:38.630 align:middle line:90%


00:34:38.630 --> 00:34:41.540 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: OK, often--
OK, how do I phrase it.

00:34:41.540 --> 00:34:43.889 align:middle line:90%
What if the only--

00:34:43.889 --> 00:34:46.820 align:middle line:84%
all the questions
the baby hears is

00:34:46.820 --> 00:34:51.290 align:middle line:84%
like, "Who did this," "Who
did that," "What happened?"

00:34:51.290 --> 00:34:54.130 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
"What happened," yeah.

00:34:54.130 --> 00:34:57.083 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Where it
could plausibly--

00:34:57.083 --> 00:34:58.250 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Be in situ.

00:34:58.250 --> 00:34:59.060 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Be in situ.

00:34:59.060 --> 00:34:59.977 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:34:59.977 --> 00:35:06.860 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Could they potentially
realize, or like think maybe

00:35:06.860 --> 00:35:08.148 align:middle line:90%
it's not in situ.

00:35:08.148 --> 00:35:09.440 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Nice question.

00:35:09.440 --> 00:35:10.980 align:middle line:84%
So everything I've
told you so far

00:35:10.980 --> 00:35:15.440 align:middle line:84%
makes it sound like you
could fool babies, in theory,

00:35:15.440 --> 00:35:16.850 align:middle line:90%
in that way.

00:35:16.850 --> 00:35:19.870 align:middle line:84%
Like you say, it's hard to
test because, in fact, I

00:35:19.870 --> 00:35:24.170 align:middle line:84%
mean you probably can't
recruit parents to agree never

00:35:24.170 --> 00:35:26.360 align:middle line:84%
to ask questions like "What
do you want for lunch?"

00:35:26.360 --> 00:35:29.060 align:middle line:90%


00:35:29.060 --> 00:35:31.760 align:middle line:84%
But you're absolutely right
that you could imagine children

00:35:31.760 --> 00:35:34.550 align:middle line:84%
going through, probably,
a pretty brief stage

00:35:34.550 --> 00:35:39.620 align:middle line:84%
where they thought that
was a viable option.

00:35:39.620 --> 00:35:44.190 align:middle line:84%
In a way, your question gets
at something else that I said,

00:35:44.190 --> 00:35:47.030 align:middle line:84%
which is languages--
so I've said there are

00:35:47.030 --> 00:35:48.380 align:middle line:90%
different kinds of languages.

00:35:48.380 --> 00:35:53.335 align:middle line:84%
There are wh-in-situ languages,
there are wh-moving languages,

00:35:53.335 --> 00:35:54.710 align:middle line:84%
and-- this was
Faith's question--

00:35:54.710 --> 00:35:57.170 align:middle line:84%
there are languages that
allow either option.

00:35:57.170 --> 00:36:00.880 align:middle line:90%
That's also a kind of language.

00:36:00.880 --> 00:36:04.370 align:middle line:84%
And that's all I said
about that right.

00:36:04.370 --> 00:36:07.598 align:middle line:84%
You could now ask,
does that just

00:36:07.598 --> 00:36:09.140 align:middle line:84%
mean that if I want
to learn English,

00:36:09.140 --> 00:36:11.510 align:middle line:84%
I must learn it's a
wh-movement language?

00:36:11.510 --> 00:36:14.060 align:middle line:84%
Or does the fact that it's
a wh-movement language

00:36:14.060 --> 00:36:16.377 align:middle line:84%
have anything to do with
anything else about English?

00:36:16.377 --> 00:36:18.710 align:middle line:84%
Is that just a parameter we
have to state about English,

00:36:18.710 --> 00:36:21.770 align:middle line:84%
or does it follow from
other stuff about English?

00:36:21.770 --> 00:36:24.248 align:middle line:84%
And that's also an
active research question,

00:36:24.248 --> 00:36:26.540 align:middle line:84%
is whether we can get away
without having to just state

00:36:26.540 --> 00:36:30.102 align:middle line:84%
those parameters, or
whether we can connect that

00:36:30.102 --> 00:36:31.310 align:middle line:90%
to other facts about English.

00:36:31.310 --> 00:36:32.960 align:middle line:84%
That would mean that an
English-speaking baby would

00:36:32.960 --> 00:36:34.377 align:middle line:84%
have other sources
of information,

00:36:34.377 --> 00:36:38.450 align:middle line:84%
possibly, besides just
actually hearing wh-questions.

00:36:38.450 --> 00:36:42.440 align:middle line:84%
So just to make it more
specific what I mean,

00:36:42.440 --> 00:36:44.450 align:middle line:84%
so here's something
which is not true.

00:36:44.450 --> 00:36:47.780 align:middle line:84%
If it turned out that every
language in which the subject

00:36:47.780 --> 00:36:51.170 align:middle line:84%
comes before the verb is a
language with wh-movement--

00:36:51.170 --> 00:36:52.260 align:middle line:90%
now, that's false.

00:36:52.260 --> 00:36:55.400 align:middle line:84%
So Mandarin is a language where
subject comes before the verb,

00:36:55.400 --> 00:36:56.690 align:middle line:90%
and it has wh-in-situ.

00:36:56.690 --> 00:36:59.053 align:middle line:84%
But imagine that it turned
out that every language where

00:36:59.053 --> 00:37:01.220 align:middle line:84%
the subject comes before
the verb is a language with

00:37:01.220 --> 00:37:03.860 align:middle line:84%
wh-movement. , Then an
English-speaking baby

00:37:03.860 --> 00:37:05.892 align:middle line:84%
wouldn't have to hear
any wh-questions at all.

00:37:05.892 --> 00:37:07.850 align:middle line:84%
They'd just have to hear
that the subject comes

00:37:07.850 --> 00:37:10.017 align:middle line:84%
before the verb, and then
they would be like, oh, it

00:37:10.017 --> 00:37:11.630 align:middle line:90%
must be a wh-movement language.

00:37:11.630 --> 00:37:13.970 align:middle line:84%
So if it were possible to
connect the fact that English

00:37:13.970 --> 00:37:16.580 align:middle line:84%
has wh-movement to some
other fact about English,

00:37:16.580 --> 00:37:19.400 align:middle line:84%
then babies would have
other sources of data.

00:37:19.400 --> 00:37:21.530 align:middle line:84%
Now, like I say, that
theory that I just

00:37:21.530 --> 00:37:23.820 align:middle line:84%
offered as a toy theory,
that's not the right theory.

00:37:23.820 --> 00:37:25.945 align:middle line:84%
So we have to work harder
to find the right theory.

00:37:25.945 --> 00:37:29.040 align:middle line:84%
But that's the kind of theory
that would give you more data,

00:37:29.040 --> 00:37:31.670 align:middle line:90%
yeah Yeah?

00:37:31.670 --> 00:37:35.300 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So you said babies
never consider that it could be

00:37:35.300 --> 00:37:39.083 align:middle line:84%
[INAUDIBLE] oh, a
maximum of two--

00:37:39.083 --> 00:37:40.000 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:37:40.000 --> 00:37:41.560 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: --that
move to the left.

00:37:41.560 --> 00:37:44.240 align:middle line:84%
But how do we know that
it's not just like--

00:37:44.240 --> 00:37:47.740 align:middle line:84%
that's not the data
that the baby would get?

00:37:47.740 --> 00:37:53.460 align:middle line:84%
A baby that has spent eight
or nine months just taking

00:37:53.460 --> 00:37:54.433 align:middle line:90%
in sound.

00:37:54.433 --> 00:37:55.350 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Data?

00:37:55.350 --> 00:37:57.300 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And before
they even speak a sound.

00:37:57.300 --> 00:37:58.217 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:37:58.217 --> 00:38:01.570 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So how do we
know that it's just like,

00:38:01.570 --> 00:38:05.050 align:middle line:84%
oh, this was not
part of what was

00:38:05.050 --> 00:38:07.730 align:middle line:90%
presented as data to the baby?

00:38:07.730 --> 00:38:09.520 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So
I don't know if this

00:38:09.520 --> 00:38:11.228 align:middle line:84%
is going to address
your question or not,

00:38:11.228 --> 00:38:13.300 align:middle line:90%
but let me try.

00:38:13.300 --> 00:38:17.680 align:middle line:84%
No matter how long we let the
baby spend learning Bulgarian,

00:38:17.680 --> 00:38:20.860 align:middle line:84%
no matter how much
Bulgarian they hear,

00:38:20.860 --> 00:38:24.160 align:middle line:84%
there's some maximum
number of words

00:38:24.160 --> 00:38:26.020 align:middle line:90%
they heard in a wh-question.

00:38:26.020 --> 00:38:30.040 align:middle line:84%
It might not be two-- it might
be three, it might be four--

00:38:30.040 --> 00:38:32.230 align:middle line:90%
but it's some number.

00:38:32.230 --> 00:38:36.940 align:middle line:84%
And so you might have expected
that there would be variation

00:38:36.940 --> 00:38:41.290 align:middle line:84%
among Bulgarians because any
number, whatever the number is,

00:38:41.290 --> 00:38:43.780 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't uniquely
pick out a grammar.

00:38:43.780 --> 00:38:46.810 align:middle line:84%
There's always, actually,
infinitely many possible

00:38:46.810 --> 00:38:47.380 align:middle line:90%
grammars.

00:38:47.380 --> 00:38:49.660 align:middle line:84%
So if the number that
they heard was n,

00:38:49.660 --> 00:38:51.940 align:middle line:84%
if that's the maximum
number, then it

00:38:51.940 --> 00:38:53.140 align:middle line:90%
could be "Move them all."

00:38:53.140 --> 00:38:55.255 align:middle line:84%
It could be "Move them
all, up to a maximum of n."

00:38:55.255 --> 00:38:57.940 align:middle line:84%
It could be "Move them all
up to a maximum of n plus 1,"

00:38:57.940 --> 00:38:59.560 align:middle line:90%
or n plus 2, or plus 3.

00:38:59.560 --> 00:39:01.990 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, it could be,
again, infinitely

00:39:01.990 --> 00:39:03.100 align:middle line:90%
many possible grammars.

00:39:03.100 --> 00:39:05.253 align:middle line:84%
And so you might
have hoped, you might

00:39:05.253 --> 00:39:06.670 align:middle line:84%
have expected,
that there would be

00:39:06.670 --> 00:39:09.550 align:middle line:84%
lots of different adult
Bulgarian grammars.

00:39:09.550 --> 00:39:11.680 align:middle line:84%
But there aren't;
there's only one.

00:39:11.680 --> 00:39:14.800 align:middle line:90%
It's "move them all."

00:39:14.800 --> 00:39:17.132 align:middle line:84%
Is that getting
at your question?

00:39:17.132 --> 00:39:18.190 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Probably, yeah.

00:39:18.190 --> 00:39:19.940 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So
it just doesn't matter

00:39:19.940 --> 00:39:21.640 align:middle line:90%
how much data the babies have.

00:39:21.640 --> 00:39:24.490 align:middle line:84%
It's never enough to
uniquely determine

00:39:24.490 --> 00:39:26.500 align:middle line:90%
what the grammar ought to be.

00:39:26.500 --> 00:39:29.980 align:middle line:84%
It's got to be the data plus
something in the baby's head

00:39:29.980 --> 00:39:34.570 align:middle line:84%
that says, the only options
are don't move any of them,

00:39:34.570 --> 00:39:36.700 align:middle line:84%
move one of them,
or move them all.

00:39:36.700 --> 00:39:40.900 align:middle line:84%
Those three are the only options
because no matter how much data

00:39:40.900 --> 00:39:43.240 align:middle line:84%
they have, they'll
never uniquely determine

00:39:43.240 --> 00:39:46.700 align:middle line:84%
the right data,
the right grammar.

00:39:46.700 --> 00:39:47.540 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:39:47.540 --> 00:39:49.580 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I don't know
a lot about this topic,

00:39:49.580 --> 00:39:52.202 align:middle line:84%
but isn't there some
merit in studying

00:39:52.202 --> 00:39:55.646 align:middle line:84%
constructed languages and the
wh-movements that exist there?

00:39:55.646 --> 00:39:58.030 align:middle line:84%
Because then you
have a lot of liberty

00:39:58.030 --> 00:40:00.743 align:middle line:84%
and get to put it wherever
you want, I would imagine?

00:40:00.743 --> 00:40:01.660 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:40:01.660 --> 00:40:05.246 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And so you could
ask, is that consistent

00:40:05.246 --> 00:40:08.485 align:middle line:84%
with the order in
any [INAUDIBLE]..

00:40:08.485 --> 00:40:10.610 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So I don't
know if this is getting

00:40:10.610 --> 00:40:13.310 align:middle line:84%
at your question either, and you
should stop me if it doesn't.

00:40:13.310 --> 00:40:14.720 align:middle line:84%
No, you should just
let me ramble on,

00:40:14.720 --> 00:40:16.303 align:middle line:84%
and then you should
tell me afterwards

00:40:16.303 --> 00:40:17.870 align:middle line:90%
whether it did or not.

00:40:17.870 --> 00:40:19.410 align:middle line:90%
That's what you should do.

00:40:19.410 --> 00:40:22.580 align:middle line:84%
There are experiments
in which people

00:40:22.580 --> 00:40:25.700 align:middle line:84%
try to find out--
in which people

00:40:25.700 --> 00:40:27.170 align:middle line:90%
ask people to learn languages.

00:40:27.170 --> 00:40:28.940 align:middle line:84%
So they'll give
people languages,

00:40:28.940 --> 00:40:32.030 align:middle line:84%
and they'll find out
things like, if I give you

00:40:32.030 --> 00:40:34.940 align:middle line:84%
a language that is a
possible human language,

00:40:34.940 --> 00:40:37.580 align:middle line:84%
are you faster at learning
that kind of language

00:40:37.580 --> 00:40:39.680 align:middle line:84%
than you would be if I
give you a language that's

00:40:39.680 --> 00:40:41.298 align:middle line:90%
not a possible human language?

00:40:41.298 --> 00:40:43.340 align:middle line:84%
So if I give you a language
in which the rule, is

00:40:43.340 --> 00:40:46.250 align:middle line:84%
move all your wh-phrases,
how are you at learning that

00:40:46.250 --> 00:40:47.690 align:middle line:84%
as opposed to a
language in which

00:40:47.690 --> 00:40:49.610 align:middle line:84%
the rule is, move all
your wh-phrases up

00:40:49.610 --> 00:40:51.110 align:middle line:90%
to a maximum of two?

00:40:51.110 --> 00:40:54.020 align:middle line:84%
And I don't know whether
that kind of experiment

00:40:54.020 --> 00:40:55.770 align:middle line:84%
has been done for this
particular problem.

00:40:55.770 --> 00:40:57.520 align:middle line:84%
But what we would hope
is that, yeah, it's

00:40:57.520 --> 00:40:59.000 align:middle line:84%
harder to learn
the languages where

00:40:59.000 --> 00:41:01.370 align:middle line:84%
it's up to a maximum
of two, the languages--

00:41:01.370 --> 00:41:03.500 align:middle line:84%
people do do this
kind of experiment.

00:41:03.500 --> 00:41:05.118 align:middle line:84%
The other kind of
natural experiment

00:41:05.118 --> 00:41:07.160 align:middle line:84%
that's been done-- this
is a lot more anecdotal--

00:41:07.160 --> 00:41:11.000 align:middle line:84%
there's, what are people
like this called now?

00:41:11.000 --> 00:41:17.660 align:middle line:84%
There's a savant, so someone
whose mental processes

00:41:17.660 --> 00:41:21.140 align:middle line:90%
are mostly very slow.

00:41:21.140 --> 00:41:25.310 align:middle line:84%
He needs a lot help
with daily life.

00:41:25.310 --> 00:41:27.560 align:middle line:84%
I think he does live by
himself, but he has caregivers

00:41:27.560 --> 00:41:29.490 align:middle line:90%
who come and help him out.

00:41:29.490 --> 00:41:31.540 align:middle line:84%
But he's a genius at
learning languages.

00:41:31.540 --> 00:41:33.773 align:middle line:84%
And so I don't
know his real name,

00:41:33.773 --> 00:41:35.690 align:middle line:84%
but his name in the
literature is Christopher.

00:41:35.690 --> 00:41:37.550 align:middle line:90%
He's British.

00:41:37.550 --> 00:41:41.480 align:middle line:84%
And Christopher loves learning
languages, does it for fun.

00:41:41.480 --> 00:41:43.730 align:middle line:84%
You can give him grammars,
and he will learn languages

00:41:43.730 --> 00:41:44.300 align:middle line:90%
immediately.

00:41:44.300 --> 00:41:46.160 align:middle line:84%
It takes him a day,
and suddenly, he

00:41:46.160 --> 00:41:47.350 align:middle line:90%
can read and write.

00:41:47.350 --> 00:41:51.470 align:middle line:84%
And he's not good at
speaking the languages

00:41:51.470 --> 00:41:54.050 align:middle line:84%
partly because he
learns by reading books

00:41:54.050 --> 00:41:56.150 align:middle line:84%
but he can learn to
read and write languages

00:41:56.150 --> 00:41:57.710 align:middle line:90%
extremely quickly.

00:41:57.710 --> 00:41:59.240 align:middle line:84%
And they have done
some experiments

00:41:59.240 --> 00:42:01.010 align:middle line:84%
like this with Christopher
where you give Christopher

00:42:01.010 --> 00:42:03.230 align:middle line:84%
languages that are not
possible languages to learn,

00:42:03.230 --> 00:42:06.320 align:middle line:84%
and he can't do
that, it turns out.

00:42:06.320 --> 00:42:09.230 align:middle line:84%
So he can learn
languages as long

00:42:09.230 --> 00:42:12.450 align:middle line:84%
as they are languages that
the rules of universal grammar

00:42:12.450 --> 00:42:12.950 align:middle line:90%
allow.

00:42:12.950 --> 00:42:14.408 align:middle line:84%
Now, again, I don't
know if they've

00:42:14.408 --> 00:42:17.942 align:middle line:84%
tried "Move all your wh-phrases
up to a maximum of two"

00:42:17.942 --> 00:42:18.650 align:middle line:90%
with Christopher.

00:42:18.650 --> 00:42:21.200 align:middle line:84%
But that's another kind of
experiment that's been done.

00:42:21.200 --> 00:42:21.770 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:42:21.770 --> 00:42:23.660 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How many
languages will Christopher

00:42:23.660 --> 00:42:26.322 align:middle line:84%
have learned by the
time [INAUDIBLE]??

00:42:26.322 --> 00:42:28.990 align:middle line:84%
Because there's
maybe a possibility

00:42:28.990 --> 00:42:31.690 align:middle line:84%
that Christopher
unconsciously figured it out

00:42:31.690 --> 00:42:35.520 align:middle line:84%
that it has to be
none, one, or all.

00:42:35.520 --> 00:42:37.980 align:middle line:84%
That's not from
Christopher's experience--

00:42:37.980 --> 00:42:39.480 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
His wide experience

00:42:39.480 --> 00:42:41.430 align:middle line:90%
with previous languages?

00:42:41.430 --> 00:42:45.990 align:middle line:84%
So all I can say is that they
have given him languages--

00:42:45.990 --> 00:42:47.530 align:middle line:90%
they have worried about this.

00:42:47.530 --> 00:42:49.800 align:middle line:84%
So they have given him
languages-- thank you

00:42:49.800 --> 00:42:51.410 align:middle line:90%
for figuring out the doors.

00:42:51.410 --> 00:42:54.150 align:middle line:84%
[LAUGHS] They have
given him languages

00:42:54.150 --> 00:42:58.710 align:middle line:84%
that differ from the languages
he's learned in some regard.

00:42:58.710 --> 00:43:00.300 align:middle line:84%
Most of the languages
he's learned

00:43:00.300 --> 00:43:02.170 align:middle line:90%
are various languages of Europe.

00:43:02.170 --> 00:43:05.440 align:middle line:90%
He's done like 20 or 30 by now.

00:43:05.440 --> 00:43:09.810 align:middle line:84%
And so they have given him
languages that differ from

00:43:09.810 --> 00:43:12.570 align:middle line:84%
the languages that he's known
to find out whether he can learn

00:43:12.570 --> 00:43:14.910 align:middle line:84%
them just as quickly
as languages that--

00:43:14.910 --> 00:43:17.160 align:middle line:84%
because they also get, when
they do these experiments,

00:43:17.160 --> 00:43:19.830 align:middle line:84%
they'll give him a language
that does obey some rule

00:43:19.830 --> 00:43:22.635 align:middle line:84%
of universal grammar and
another language that doesn't.

00:43:22.635 --> 00:43:24.510 align:middle line:84%
But you're absolutely
right, that's something

00:43:24.510 --> 00:43:25.635 align:middle line:90%
they have to watch out for.

00:43:25.635 --> 00:43:28.290 align:middle line:84%
And all I know is that they
have been careful about that.

00:43:28.290 --> 00:43:30.770 align:middle line:84%
Whether they've been careful
enough, I can't swear.

00:43:30.770 --> 00:43:33.232 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's a good point.

00:43:33.232 --> 00:43:34.940 align:middle line:84%
Christopher loves
doing this, by the way.

00:43:34.940 --> 00:43:37.232 align:middle line:84%
This is not a hardship they're
imposing on Christopher.

00:43:37.232 --> 00:43:39.890 align:middle line:84%
This is one of his big joys in
life, is learning languages.

00:43:39.890 --> 00:43:45.285 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, OK.

00:43:45.285 --> 00:43:46.160 align:middle line:90%
[? Let me ?] back in?

00:43:46.160 --> 00:43:47.360 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:43:47.360 --> 00:43:49.650 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, questions about this?

00:43:49.650 --> 00:43:53.840 align:middle line:90%
OK, all right.

00:43:53.840 --> 00:43:57.350 align:middle line:84%
All right, so that's it for
wh-questions, at least for now.

00:43:57.350 --> 00:44:00.080 align:middle line:84%
Are there any
wh-question questions

00:44:00.080 --> 00:44:03.547 align:middle line:84%
before we put that aside
and go on to something else?

00:44:03.547 --> 00:44:04.617 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:44:04.617 --> 00:44:06.950 align:middle line:84%
We have spent a lot of time
talking about the projection

00:44:06.950 --> 00:44:07.910 align:middle line:90%
principle--

00:44:07.910 --> 00:44:11.960 align:middle line:84%
this idea that if you have a
head, and it selects something,

00:44:11.960 --> 00:44:14.960 align:middle line:84%
that thing needs
to be its sister.

00:44:14.960 --> 00:44:17.570 align:middle line:84%
Then, I've incautiously
put the verb "put"

00:44:17.570 --> 00:44:20.245 align:middle line:84%
in this example where, I don't
know, it needs two sisters,

00:44:20.245 --> 00:44:21.620 align:middle line:84%
or we need to say
something else.

00:44:21.620 --> 00:44:24.140 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, there are strict
locality restrictions

00:44:24.140 --> 00:44:26.780 align:middle line:84%
on the relationship between
the head and the thing

00:44:26.780 --> 00:44:28.220 align:middle line:90%
that it selects.

00:44:28.220 --> 00:44:31.070 align:middle line:84%
So we have lexical entries
for verbs like "devour,"

00:44:31.070 --> 00:44:34.340 align:middle line:84%
and, "put," and "faint," that
say things like "'devour' needs

00:44:34.340 --> 00:44:37.095 align:middle line:84%
a sister that's a noun phrase"--
and "'put' needs to combine

00:44:37.095 --> 00:44:39.470 align:middle line:84%
with a noun phrase and also
with a prepositional phrase,"

00:44:39.470 --> 00:44:41.262 align:middle line:90%
and "'faint' is intransitive."

00:44:41.262 --> 00:44:43.220 align:middle line:84%
It had better not combine
with anything at all.

00:44:43.220 --> 00:44:47.600 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, those are all things
that we've been saying.

00:44:47.600 --> 00:44:49.790 align:middle line:84%
And what we've been
saying is, if you

00:44:49.790 --> 00:44:51.860 align:middle line:84%
have a head that doesn't
select for a sister,

00:44:51.860 --> 00:44:53.790 align:middle line:84%
well, then, it doesn't
have a sister--

00:44:53.790 --> 00:44:54.800 align:middle line:90%
the end.

00:44:54.800 --> 00:44:58.340 align:middle line:84%
So "he fainted," "faint,"
it's an intransitive verb.

00:44:58.340 --> 00:45:00.260 align:middle line:84%
Doesn't need a sister,
doesn't get one,

00:45:00.260 --> 00:45:02.833 align:middle line:90%
and that's the end, OK?

00:45:02.833 --> 00:45:05.250 align:middle line:84%
Unless you add an adjunct, but
it doesn't get an argument.

00:45:05.250 --> 00:45:07.870 align:middle line:90%


00:45:07.870 --> 00:45:10.900 align:middle line:84%
But there's nothing comparable
in subject position.

00:45:10.900 --> 00:45:12.390 align:middle line:84%
So when we talk
about verbs being

00:45:12.390 --> 00:45:16.380 align:middle line:84%
transitive or intransitive,
we're talking about objects.

00:45:16.380 --> 00:45:17.790 align:middle line:84%
So people have
been talking about

00:45:17.790 --> 00:45:19.230 align:middle line:84%
transitive and
intransitive verbs,

00:45:19.230 --> 00:45:20.903 align:middle line:90%
literally, for centuries.

00:45:20.903 --> 00:45:22.320 align:middle line:84%
It's an old
observation that there

00:45:22.320 --> 00:45:24.903 align:middle line:84%
are verbs that need objects and
verbs that don't need objects.

00:45:24.903 --> 00:45:27.060 align:middle line:84%
But there's nothing comparable
in subject position.

00:45:27.060 --> 00:45:28.643 align:middle line:84%
That is, people don't
talk about verbs

00:45:28.643 --> 00:45:31.020 align:middle line:84%
that need subjects and verbs
that don't need subjects.

00:45:31.020 --> 00:45:33.990 align:middle line:90%
Verbs just always have subjects.

00:45:33.990 --> 00:45:40.110 align:middle line:84%
So and sometimes, the
subjects are a little sketchy.

00:45:40.110 --> 00:45:45.000 align:middle line:84%
I mean, so we say things like
"It rained," and it's not

00:45:45.000 --> 00:45:47.520 align:middle line:84%
too clear what "it"
is supposed to be.

00:45:47.520 --> 00:45:49.140 align:middle line:90%
You can't be anything else.

00:45:49.140 --> 00:45:52.290 align:middle line:84%
You don't get to say,
"the sky rained,"

00:45:52.290 --> 00:45:55.440 align:middle line:90%
or "God rained," or whatever.

00:45:55.440 --> 00:45:56.970 align:middle line:90%
It sort of needs to be "it."

00:45:56.970 --> 00:45:59.962 align:middle line:90%


00:45:59.962 --> 00:46:02.170 align:middle line:84%
And there are other kinds
of examples like that, too.

00:46:02.170 --> 00:46:04.770 align:middle line:84%
So "It seems that
John has died,"

00:46:04.770 --> 00:46:08.010 align:middle line:84%
if I say that to you,
you're not going to say,

00:46:08.010 --> 00:46:10.380 align:middle line:90%
"What seems that John has died?

00:46:10.380 --> 00:46:11.760 align:middle line:90%
What is 'it'?

00:46:11.760 --> 00:46:14.050 align:middle line:90%
What do you mean by 'it' there?"

00:46:14.050 --> 00:46:15.480 align:middle line:90%
I don't mean anything at all.

00:46:15.480 --> 00:46:16.938 align:middle line:90%
"It" is just there--

00:46:16.938 --> 00:46:19.230 align:middle line:84%
it's as though it's there so
that the sentence can have

00:46:19.230 --> 00:46:23.220 align:middle line:84%
a subject, even though
the subject doesn't

00:46:23.220 --> 00:46:27.340 align:middle line:84%
contribute anything to the
meaning of the sentence.

00:46:27.340 --> 00:46:33.410 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, if you compare-- here's
a short, kind of sad story.

00:46:33.410 --> 00:46:36.460 align:middle line:84%
So "It squeezed John, and it
seems that John has died."

00:46:36.460 --> 00:46:39.420 align:middle line:90%
Is anybody here named John?

00:46:39.420 --> 00:46:39.920 align:middle line:90%
Good!

00:46:39.920 --> 00:46:41.330 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:46:41.330 --> 00:46:45.260 align:middle line:84%
So I should probably have
warned you about this early on.

00:46:45.260 --> 00:46:48.650 align:middle line:84%
Example sentences in
linguistics, I mean, they vary.

00:46:48.650 --> 00:46:51.800 align:middle line:84%
Often, you need a name, and
John is one of the classics.

00:46:51.800 --> 00:46:54.770 align:middle line:90%
Mary is another one.

00:46:54.770 --> 00:46:57.950 align:middle line:84%
So the first time I ever taught
intro to linguistics, there

00:46:57.950 --> 00:46:59.630 align:middle line:84%
was a John and a
Mary in the class.

00:46:59.630 --> 00:47:02.780 align:middle line:90%
It was very confining.

00:47:02.780 --> 00:47:04.850 align:middle line:84%
So since there's
no John, I can just

00:47:04.850 --> 00:47:06.590 align:middle line:90%
let these example sentences rip.

00:47:06.590 --> 00:47:07.460 align:middle line:90%
"It squeezed John.

00:47:07.460 --> 00:47:09.680 align:middle line:90%
It seems that John has died."

00:47:09.680 --> 00:47:12.110 align:middle line:84%
These two "it"s seem to
have a different status.

00:47:12.110 --> 00:47:15.470 align:middle line:84%
So with the first one, if
I say, "It squeezed John,"

00:47:15.470 --> 00:47:17.330 align:middle line:84%
if I say that out
of the blue, you're

00:47:17.330 --> 00:47:19.520 align:middle line:84%
entitled to wonder,
"What squeezed John?"

00:47:19.520 --> 00:47:21.320 align:middle line:90%
That's a reasonable question.

00:47:21.320 --> 00:47:24.950 align:middle line:84%
So it's some machine, or
a snake, or something.

00:47:24.950 --> 00:47:26.300 align:middle line:90%
Something squeezed John.

00:47:26.300 --> 00:47:29.030 align:middle line:84%
But if I say, "it seems
that John has died, "

00:47:29.030 --> 00:47:32.510 align:middle line:84%
even if you don't care
about John at all,

00:47:32.510 --> 00:47:34.100 align:middle line:84%
your question is
not going to be,

00:47:34.100 --> 00:47:36.140 align:middle line:90%
"What seems that John has died?"

00:47:36.140 --> 00:47:37.700 align:middle line:84%
That question doesn't
make any sense.

00:47:37.700 --> 00:47:40.540 align:middle line:90%


00:47:40.540 --> 00:47:42.665 align:middle line:84%
So these two "it"s seem to
have a different status.

00:47:42.665 --> 00:47:45.716 align:middle line:90%


00:47:45.716 --> 00:47:49.260 align:middle line:84%
There's a classic way of
handling this kind of fact,

00:47:49.260 --> 00:47:52.370 align:middle line:84%
which is to offer what's
called the Extended Projection

00:47:52.370 --> 00:47:55.438 align:middle line:90%
Principle, which--

00:47:55.438 --> 00:47:57.230 align:middle line:84%
yeah, so the Extended
Projection Principle.

00:47:57.230 --> 00:47:58.772 align:middle line:84%
So you know the
Projection Principle,

00:47:58.772 --> 00:48:00.860 align:middle line:84%
which says that heads,
when they select,

00:48:00.860 --> 00:48:03.020 align:middle line:84%
they need to select things
that are their sisters,

00:48:03.020 --> 00:48:06.360 align:middle line:84%
or they need their things they
select to be close to them.

00:48:06.360 --> 00:48:08.990 align:middle line:84%
The Extended Projection
Principle says, there

00:48:08.990 --> 00:48:10.730 align:middle line:90%
needs to be something here.

00:48:10.730 --> 00:48:13.700 align:middle line:90%
So TP needs to have a specifier.

00:48:13.700 --> 00:48:17.210 align:middle line:84%
You can't have a sentence that
doesn't have a subject in it.

00:48:17.210 --> 00:48:20.630 align:middle line:84%
Calling it the Extended
Projection Principle,

00:48:20.630 --> 00:48:23.040 align:middle line:90%
this was Noam Chomsky's idea.

00:48:23.040 --> 00:48:28.015 align:middle line:84%
And so I feel secure in telling
you that it's an obvious hack.

00:48:28.015 --> 00:48:29.390 align:middle line:84%
The Extended
Projection Principle

00:48:29.390 --> 00:48:32.000 align:middle line:84%
has nothing to do with
the Projection Principle.

00:48:32.000 --> 00:48:33.740 align:middle line:84%
He really just called
it that, I think,

00:48:33.740 --> 00:48:35.750 align:middle line:84%
in order to paint a
big red target on it.

00:48:35.750 --> 00:48:38.720 align:middle line:84%
He's like, why the heck
does it work this way,

00:48:38.720 --> 00:48:41.630 align:middle line:84%
that there has to be something
in the specifier of TP?

00:48:41.630 --> 00:48:43.370 align:middle line:84%
Linguists, go and try
to figure out what

00:48:43.370 --> 00:48:45.290 align:middle line:90%
the heck is going on there.

00:48:45.290 --> 00:48:46.790 align:middle line:84%
So the Extended
Projection Principle

00:48:46.790 --> 00:48:51.077 align:middle line:84%
says the specifier of TP-- this
is a word I've used before,

00:48:51.077 --> 00:48:53.660 align:middle line:84%
and I hope I've defined it, but
if I haven't, let me define it

00:48:53.660 --> 00:48:55.490 align:middle line:84%
for you-- the
specifier of TP, there

00:48:55.490 --> 00:48:57.320 align:middle line:84%
has to be something in
the specifier of TP.

00:48:57.320 --> 00:48:58.850 align:middle line:90%
What's the specifier of TP?

00:48:58.850 --> 00:49:02.360 align:middle line:84%
It's a daughter of TP-- so
a daughter of the maximum

00:49:02.360 --> 00:49:03.830 align:middle line:90%
projection--

00:49:03.830 --> 00:49:05.930 align:middle line:90%
that doesn't have the label T--

00:49:05.930 --> 00:49:08.630 align:middle line:84%
so it's that thing that the
red arrow is pointing to--

00:49:08.630 --> 00:49:11.150 align:middle line:84%
and it's not selected,
or at least it

00:49:11.150 --> 00:49:13.020 align:middle line:84%
doesn't have to be
selected, by anything.

00:49:13.020 --> 00:49:15.620 align:middle line:84%
So here's another place where
there has to be something.

00:49:15.620 --> 00:49:18.710 align:middle line:84%
If you have a transitive
verb, the transitive verb

00:49:18.710 --> 00:49:21.385 align:middle line:90%
has to have a sister, an object.

00:49:21.385 --> 00:49:23.510 align:middle line:84%
If you have a verb that
selects for something else,

00:49:23.510 --> 00:49:25.580 align:middle line:84%
like "depends" selects
for a prepositional phrase

00:49:25.580 --> 00:49:28.560 align:middle line:84%
headed by "on", and it has to
have that prepositional phrase.

00:49:28.560 --> 00:49:29.560 align:middle line:90%
Here's another position.

00:49:29.560 --> 00:49:31.430 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't seem to have
anything to do with selection.

00:49:31.430 --> 00:49:32.888 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't matter
what the verb is.

00:49:32.888 --> 00:49:35.510 align:middle line:84%
There just has to be
something in subject position.

00:49:35.510 --> 00:49:38.450 align:middle line:84%
So it's called the Extended
Projection Principle.

00:49:38.450 --> 00:49:43.400 align:middle line:84%
Calling it a principle is
dignifying it too much.

00:49:43.400 --> 00:49:47.120 align:middle line:84%
And what we're seeing is that
in sentences where there isn't

00:49:47.120 --> 00:49:50.120 align:middle line:84%
anything else to satisfy the
Extended Projection Principle

00:49:50.120 --> 00:49:52.370 align:middle line:90%
with, then you insert "it."

00:49:52.370 --> 00:49:54.950 align:middle line:90%
You can insert "it."

00:49:54.950 --> 00:49:57.930 align:middle line:84%
That's what you're doing in
"It seems that John has died."

00:49:57.930 --> 00:50:00.233 align:middle line:84%
That is, you have
this subject position.

00:50:00.233 --> 00:50:01.650 align:middle line:84%
There needs to be
something there,

00:50:01.650 --> 00:50:04.580 align:middle line:84%
and so you put this
meaningless "it" there.

00:50:04.580 --> 00:50:06.710 align:middle line:84%
This is why you can't
ask questions like, "What

00:50:06.710 --> 00:50:08.090 align:middle line:90%
seems that John has died?"

00:50:08.090 --> 00:50:10.700 align:middle line:84%
Because there's nothing
meaningful there.

00:50:10.700 --> 00:50:15.573 align:middle line:84%
That meaningless version of
"it" is called an expletive.

00:50:15.573 --> 00:50:17.240 align:middle line:84%
There are other names
for it, but that's

00:50:17.240 --> 00:50:19.680 align:middle line:90%
what we usually call it.

00:50:19.680 --> 00:50:22.050 align:middle line:90%
So it doesn't mean anything.

00:50:22.050 --> 00:50:24.950 align:middle line:84%
It's just there to satisfy the
EPP, the Extended Projection

00:50:24.950 --> 00:50:28.100 align:middle line:90%
Principle.

00:50:28.100 --> 00:50:31.470 align:middle line:84%
I think I may say
this on a later slide.

00:50:31.470 --> 00:50:35.760 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, we've just
gone through saying

00:50:35.760 --> 00:50:38.550 align:middle line:90%
languages vary in various ways.

00:50:38.550 --> 00:50:40.290 align:middle line:84%
This is a place
where languages vary.

00:50:40.290 --> 00:50:42.000 align:middle line:84%
English has the Extended
Projection Principle.

00:50:42.000 --> 00:50:43.050 align:middle line:90%
There are other languages.

00:50:43.050 --> 00:50:44.640 align:middle line:84%
French has the Extended
Projection principle

00:50:44.640 --> 00:50:45.610 align:middle line:90%
There are some others.

00:50:45.610 --> 00:50:48.193 align:middle line:84%
But there are many languages--
in fact, maybe most languages--

00:50:48.193 --> 00:50:49.630 align:middle line:90%
that don't have this.

00:50:49.630 --> 00:50:52.230 align:middle line:84%
So there's languages that
are perfectly happy to start

00:50:52.230 --> 00:50:53.770 align:middle line:90%
the sentence with the verb.

00:50:53.770 --> 00:50:55.958 align:middle line:84%
So if you're working
on a language,

00:50:55.958 --> 00:50:58.500 align:middle line:84%
yeah, if you speak Spanish, then
you're thinking to yourself,

00:50:58.500 --> 00:51:00.060 align:middle line:84%
wait, it's fine to
say things like,

00:51:00.060 --> 00:51:03.690 align:middle line:84%
"Seems that John has died" in
lots of languages of the world.

00:51:03.690 --> 00:51:06.770 align:middle line:90%


00:51:06.770 --> 00:51:09.270 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, here's another point
of cross-linguistic variation.

00:51:09.270 --> 00:51:11.100 align:middle line:84%
There are languages
like English that have

00:51:11.100 --> 00:51:12.802 align:middle line:90%
this interesting restriction.

00:51:12.802 --> 00:51:16.580 align:middle line:90%


00:51:16.580 --> 00:51:19.028 align:middle line:90%
OK, now here's another--

00:51:19.028 --> 00:51:21.320 align:middle line:84%
I'm telling you about the
Extended Protection Principle

00:51:21.320 --> 00:51:24.660 align:middle line:84%
because I want to introduce you
to another kind of movement.

00:51:24.660 --> 00:51:26.720 align:middle line:84%
So we've talked
about movement, and I

00:51:26.720 --> 00:51:29.330 align:middle line:90%
want to show you another kind.

00:51:29.330 --> 00:51:31.160 align:middle line:90%
Here's another kind.

00:51:31.160 --> 00:51:34.070 align:middle line:84%
We can say things like
"The snake squeezed John,"

00:51:34.070 --> 00:51:36.200 align:middle line:84%
or "I put the
kumquats in a bowl."

00:51:36.200 --> 00:51:40.860 align:middle line:84%
And then, so let's concentrate
on "The snake squeezed John."

00:51:40.860 --> 00:51:43.800 align:middle line:84%
"Squeeze" looks like
it's a transitive verb,

00:51:43.800 --> 00:51:46.080 align:middle line:84%
or at least it has a life
as a transitive verb.

00:51:46.080 --> 00:51:48.570 align:middle line:84%
"John" is being
selected by "squeeze."

00:51:48.570 --> 00:51:51.990 align:middle line:84%
It's the thing that's
getting squeezed.

00:51:51.990 --> 00:51:54.720 align:middle line:84%
Or similarly, in "I put
the kumquats in the bowl,"

00:51:54.720 --> 00:51:57.700 align:middle line:84%
"put" is a verb that
selects for an object.

00:51:57.700 --> 00:51:59.680 align:middle line:84%
It also selects for a
prepositional phrase.

00:51:59.680 --> 00:52:01.540 align:middle line:90%
That's what we've been saying.

00:52:01.540 --> 00:52:04.420 align:middle line:84%
But then, there are these
other forms, these passives--

00:52:04.420 --> 00:52:08.200 align:middle line:84%
"John was squeezed," and "The
kumquats were put in a bowl."

00:52:08.200 --> 00:52:11.770 align:middle line:84%
And here, we're in
a similar situation

00:52:11.770 --> 00:52:14.440 align:middle line:84%
to the situation we were
in when I first showed you

00:52:14.440 --> 00:52:16.060 align:middle line:90%
"What did Mary write?"

00:52:16.060 --> 00:52:25.100 align:middle line:84%
where I was trying to say "What
did Mary put in the bowl?"

00:52:25.100 --> 00:52:28.822 align:middle line:84%
So with "What did
Mary put in the bowl?"

00:52:28.822 --> 00:52:30.280 align:middle line:84%
I was saying, yeah,
"put," we think

00:52:30.280 --> 00:52:32.232 align:middle line:84%
"put" has to combine
with a noun phrase.

00:52:32.232 --> 00:52:33.190 align:middle line:90%
It's a transitive verb.

00:52:33.190 --> 00:52:35.080 align:middle line:84%
You can't say, "Mary
put in the bowl."

00:52:35.080 --> 00:52:37.220 align:middle line:90%
That's not a sentence.

00:52:37.220 --> 00:52:40.610 align:middle line:84%
Why is it OK to have "put" with
no noun phrase after it here?

00:52:40.610 --> 00:52:44.050 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's because "what"
started out here and moved over

00:52:44.050 --> 00:52:46.000 align:middle line:90%
to there.

00:52:46.000 --> 00:52:48.268 align:middle line:84%
For "the kumquats
were put in a bowl,"

00:52:48.268 --> 00:52:49.810 align:middle line:84%
we're going to say
something similar.

00:52:49.810 --> 00:52:52.240 align:middle line:84%
"Put" is selecting
for an object.

00:52:52.240 --> 00:52:54.520 align:middle line:84%
In this case, it's the
kumquats, and the kumquats

00:52:54.520 --> 00:52:57.857 align:middle line:84%
are moving out of that
position into another position.

00:52:57.857 --> 00:52:59.440 align:middle line:84%
What's the position
they're moving to?

00:52:59.440 --> 00:53:00.790 align:middle line:90%
Well, they're not wh-moving.

00:53:00.790 --> 00:53:02.650 align:middle line:90%
They're not wh-words.

00:53:02.650 --> 00:53:04.010 align:middle line:90%
This isn't a question.

00:53:04.010 --> 00:53:07.130 align:middle line:84%
This is another
kind of movement.

00:53:07.130 --> 00:53:09.250 align:middle line:84%
So the same reasoning
that prompted

00:53:09.250 --> 00:53:11.680 align:middle line:84%
this to posit
wh-movement in order

00:53:11.680 --> 00:53:14.590 align:middle line:84%
to save our beliefs
about how selection works

00:53:14.590 --> 00:53:19.430 align:middle line:84%
leads us to suspect that there's
movement going on here as well.

00:53:19.430 --> 00:53:22.540 align:middle line:84%
So we'll start off with
"Was squeezed John"

00:53:22.540 --> 00:53:26.470 align:middle line:84%
or "Were put kumquats
in the bowl."

00:53:26.470 --> 00:53:30.250 align:middle line:84%
And then, the EPP is going to
say, oh, there's no subject.

00:53:30.250 --> 00:53:33.040 align:middle line:84%
So what happens with the
passive is that you get rid

00:53:33.040 --> 00:53:34.630 align:middle line:90%
of the subject.

00:53:34.630 --> 00:53:37.120 align:middle line:84%
"Was squeezed John,"
you've gotten rid

00:53:37.120 --> 00:53:38.890 align:middle line:84%
of the snake that
was squeezing John.

00:53:38.890 --> 00:53:42.312 align:middle line:84%
Or "Were put in the bowl," "Were
put the kumquats in the bowl,"

00:53:42.312 --> 00:53:44.020 align:middle line:84%
you've gotten rid of
Mary who was putting

00:53:44.020 --> 00:53:45.062 align:middle line:90%
the kumquats in the bowl.

00:53:45.062 --> 00:53:47.410 align:middle line:84%
And then, the EPP
says, oh, no, wait,

00:53:47.410 --> 00:53:49.490 align:middle line:84%
we need something
in subject position.

00:53:49.490 --> 00:53:53.180 align:middle line:84%
And so we move-- in this case
"John," or in the other case,

00:53:53.180 --> 00:53:55.147 align:middle line:84%
"the kumquats"-- into
subject positions.

00:53:55.147 --> 00:53:56.230 align:middle line:90%
So there can be a subject.

00:53:56.230 --> 00:53:58.900 align:middle line:90%
So the EPP can be satisfied.

00:53:58.900 --> 00:54:01.610 align:middle line:84%
It's a way of talking about
these kinds of examples.

00:54:01.610 --> 00:54:05.170 align:middle line:84%
So this is a new kind of
movement driven by the EPP.

00:54:05.170 --> 00:54:08.710 align:middle line:84%
It's called-- sometimes
called NP-movement.

00:54:08.710 --> 00:54:11.440 align:middle line:84%
It's been called other things,
but I'll call it NP-movement.

00:54:11.440 --> 00:54:13.870 align:middle line:84%
It always seems to
be movement of NPs.

00:54:13.870 --> 00:54:16.600 align:middle line:84%
So you take an NP like
"John," and you move it,

00:54:16.600 --> 00:54:19.480 align:middle line:84%
in this case, out
of the position

00:54:19.480 --> 00:54:22.870 align:middle line:84%
as the sister of "squeeze,"
where it was selected,

00:54:22.870 --> 00:54:25.540 align:middle line:84%
into subject position
so that the sentence can

00:54:25.540 --> 00:54:28.570 align:middle line:84%
have a subject, so that
TP can have a specifier.

00:54:28.570 --> 00:54:31.010 align:middle line:90%
It seems to be what's happening.

00:54:31.010 --> 00:54:34.780 align:middle line:84%
Let me give you some reasons to
take that analysis seriously.

00:54:34.780 --> 00:54:36.790 align:middle line:84%
But first, is the
analysis clear?

00:54:36.790 --> 00:54:41.020 align:middle line:84%
That's what we
think is happening.

00:54:41.020 --> 00:54:41.740 align:middle line:90%
Oh, sorry.

00:54:41.740 --> 00:54:43.910 align:middle line:84%
First, let me show you
another kind of NP-movement.

00:54:43.910 --> 00:54:46.720 align:middle line:84%
So those first
ones were passives.

00:54:46.720 --> 00:54:47.630 align:middle line:90%
Here's another one.

00:54:47.630 --> 00:54:51.100 align:middle line:84%
So in "It seems
that John is sick,"

00:54:51.100 --> 00:54:53.630 align:middle line:84%
there's an "it" at the
beginning of the sentence

00:54:53.630 --> 00:54:55.990 align:middle line:84%
which is the expletive
kind of "it."

00:54:55.990 --> 00:54:58.157 align:middle line:84%
So it's the kind of "it"
that doesn't mean anything.

00:54:58.157 --> 00:55:00.282 align:middle line:84%
I think it was one of the
first kinds of expletives

00:55:00.282 --> 00:55:01.210 align:middle line:90%
that I showed you.

00:55:01.210 --> 00:55:03.970 align:middle line:84%
Can't say things like, "What
seems that John is sick?"

00:55:03.970 --> 00:55:06.020 align:middle line:84%
So that "q" doesn't
mean anything.

00:55:06.020 --> 00:55:10.090 align:middle line:84%
It's just there so that
TP can have a specifier.

00:55:10.090 --> 00:55:14.525 align:middle line:84%
"John" is starting off as
the subject of "be sick."

00:55:14.525 --> 00:55:15.900 align:middle line:84%
And in the first
sentence, "John"

00:55:15.900 --> 00:55:18.030 align:middle line:84%
is staying there in subject
position for "be sick."

00:55:18.030 --> 00:55:21.510 align:middle line:84%
But in the second sentence,
"John" appears to be moving.

00:55:21.510 --> 00:55:23.377 align:middle line:84%
So it's an analysis,
and I'm going

00:55:23.377 --> 00:55:25.710 align:middle line:84%
to give you some reasons to
take this analysis seriously

00:55:25.710 --> 00:55:26.340 align:middle line:90%
in a second.

00:55:26.340 --> 00:55:29.100 align:middle line:84%
We're going to say, yeah, "John"
is still the subject of "be

00:55:29.100 --> 00:55:30.640 align:middle line:90%
sick" in the second example.

00:55:30.640 --> 00:55:35.040 align:middle line:84%
But then, he moves to satisfy
the EPP in the higher clause.

00:55:35.040 --> 00:55:37.530 align:middle line:84%
So the higher verb
needs to have a subject,

00:55:37.530 --> 00:55:39.690 align:middle line:84%
and here, for some
reason, we've elected

00:55:39.690 --> 00:55:41.130 align:middle line:90%
not to merge an expletive.

00:55:41.130 --> 00:55:43.920 align:middle line:84%
And so instead,
we're moving "John"

00:55:43.920 --> 00:55:48.245 align:middle line:84%
so that "John" can
satisfy the EPP upstairs.

00:55:48.245 --> 00:55:49.620 align:middle line:84%
Now, let me give
you some reasons

00:55:49.620 --> 00:55:52.140 align:middle line:90%
to take this analysis seriously.

00:55:52.140 --> 00:55:55.200 align:middle line:84%
So here is an argument for
the existence of NP-movement,

00:55:55.200 --> 00:55:57.253 align:middle line:90%
and it involves idioms.

00:55:57.253 --> 00:55:58.920 align:middle line:84%
So let's talk for a
second about idioms.

00:55:58.920 --> 00:56:01.530 align:middle line:84%
Idioms are always
fun to talk about.

00:56:01.530 --> 00:56:07.080 align:middle line:84%
Idioms are combinations
of words that

00:56:07.080 --> 00:56:10.410 align:middle line:84%
don't have to mean what they
appear to literally mean.

00:56:10.410 --> 00:56:14.130 align:middle line:84%
So "kick the bucket"
and "buy the farm"

00:56:14.130 --> 00:56:17.080 align:middle line:90%
are both idioms that mean "die."

00:56:17.080 --> 00:56:19.530 align:middle line:84%
So if you say "John
bought the farm"--

00:56:19.530 --> 00:56:22.973 align:middle line:84%
continuing to pick on John,
since there are no Johns here--

00:56:22.973 --> 00:56:24.390 align:middle line:84%
if you say "John
bought the farm,"

00:56:24.390 --> 00:56:27.480 align:middle line:84%
that could mean John
purchased a farm.

00:56:27.480 --> 00:56:29.460 align:middle line:90%
It has its literal meaning.

00:56:29.460 --> 00:56:32.160 align:middle line:84%
But it can also
mean, "John died."

00:56:32.160 --> 00:56:36.120 align:middle line:84%
Or, "John kicked the bucket"
can literally mean, you know,

00:56:36.120 --> 00:56:37.440 align:middle line:90%
John kicked a bucket.

00:56:37.440 --> 00:56:40.230 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, but it can also
mean that John died.

00:56:40.230 --> 00:56:41.530 align:middle line:90%
Do people have these idioms?

00:56:41.530 --> 00:56:43.130 align:middle line:84%
Are these things
that you can say?

00:56:43.130 --> 00:56:44.880 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I've never
heard "buy the farm."

00:56:44.880 --> 00:56:46.020 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: You've
never heard "buy the farm"?

00:56:46.020 --> 00:56:47.987 align:middle line:84%
Has anybody heard
"buy the farm"?

00:56:47.987 --> 00:56:48.570 align:middle line:90%
OK, all right.

00:56:48.570 --> 00:56:49.862 align:middle line:90%
So I'm not just making this up.

00:56:49.862 --> 00:56:51.067 align:middle line:90%
That's good to know.

00:56:51.067 --> 00:56:52.650 align:middle line:84%
I just have to check
for these things.

00:56:52.650 --> 00:56:56.400 align:middle line:84%
There are many, many
idioms that mean "die."

00:56:56.400 --> 00:56:59.040 align:middle line:84%
"Kick the bucket" and "buy
the farm" are two of them.

00:56:59.040 --> 00:57:01.950 align:middle line:84%
"Spill the beans," just to get
things slightly less violent,

00:57:01.950 --> 00:57:04.800 align:middle line:84%
spill the beans" means
to reveal a secret.

00:57:04.800 --> 00:57:07.423 align:middle line:84%
"Yawn in Technicolor," is
that an idiom you guys know?

00:57:07.423 --> 00:57:08.340 align:middle line:90%
"Yawn in Technicolor?"

00:57:08.340 --> 00:57:08.940 align:middle line:90%
No?

00:57:08.940 --> 00:57:12.420 align:middle line:84%
I got this one from
a book of UCLA slang.

00:57:12.420 --> 00:57:13.587 align:middle line:90%
There was a period there--

00:57:13.587 --> 00:57:14.670 align:middle line:90%
I hope they still do this.

00:57:14.670 --> 00:57:15.870 align:middle line:90%
I'm not sure they do.

00:57:15.870 --> 00:57:18.623 align:middle line:84%
The UCLA linguistics department
had an undergrad class

00:57:18.623 --> 00:57:20.040 align:middle line:84%
where the undergrads
would compile

00:57:20.040 --> 00:57:24.750 align:middle line:84%
a dictionary of undergrad slang,
and they sold the dictionary.

00:57:24.750 --> 00:57:27.640 align:middle line:84%
And you could buy the dictionary
in the university bookstore.

00:57:27.640 --> 00:57:30.240 align:middle line:90%
I bought a copy.

00:57:30.240 --> 00:57:33.060 align:middle line:84%
There were many, many--
the UCLA Slang Dictionary

00:57:33.060 --> 00:57:34.200 align:middle line:90%
is an interesting document.

00:57:34.200 --> 00:57:36.270 align:middle line:84%
There are lots and lots
of idioms that have

00:57:36.270 --> 00:57:41.160 align:middle line:90%
to do with being drunk or high.

00:57:41.160 --> 00:57:43.110 align:middle line:84%
All the idioms, the
MIT idioms that I

00:57:43.110 --> 00:57:45.450 align:middle line:84%
have to do with having
too much work to do.

00:57:45.450 --> 00:57:47.080 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:57:47.080 --> 00:57:50.730 align:middle line:84%
So I don't know if that's
true, but I get that feeling.

00:57:50.730 --> 00:57:52.420 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, the MIT
slang dictionary,

00:57:52.420 --> 00:57:54.420 align:middle line:84%
if there were an undergrad
class like that here,

00:57:54.420 --> 00:57:56.760 align:middle line:84%
I think it would be a
less entertaining read.

00:57:56.760 --> 00:57:58.140 align:middle line:90%
But I could be wrong.

00:57:58.140 --> 00:57:59.880 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, that's where
I learned this one.

00:57:59.880 --> 00:58:05.885 align:middle line:84%
I once talked about this idiom
in front of some Australians,

00:58:05.885 --> 00:58:07.260 align:middle line:84%
and they were all
like, oh, yeah,

00:58:07.260 --> 00:58:09.203 align:middle line:90%
that's an Australian idiom.

00:58:09.203 --> 00:58:10.620 align:middle line:84%
So it's possible
that that's where

00:58:10.620 --> 00:58:13.310 align:middle line:84%
it came to UCLA, that there
were Australian students who

00:58:13.310 --> 00:58:14.310 align:middle line:90%
were talking about that.

00:58:14.310 --> 00:58:16.920 align:middle line:90%
Anyway, "yawn in Technicolor"--

00:58:16.920 --> 00:58:17.940 align:middle line:90%
impress your friends.

00:58:17.940 --> 00:58:21.090 align:middle line:84%
Add this to your vocabulary,
if it ever comes up.

00:58:21.090 --> 00:58:22.690 align:middle line:90%
There are many, many idioms.

00:58:22.690 --> 00:58:24.720 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, people are
clear on what idioms are?

00:58:24.720 --> 00:58:29.100 align:middle line:84%
So these are strings of words
or structures that have literal

00:58:29.100 --> 00:58:30.920 align:middle line:84%
meanings, I guess--
"yawn in Technicolor,"

00:58:30.920 --> 00:58:33.960 align:middle line:90%
maybe not so much--

00:58:33.960 --> 00:58:36.120 align:middle line:84%
but also have these
non-literal meanings.

00:58:36.120 --> 00:58:39.720 align:middle line:84%
And the non-literal
meanings are quite--

00:58:39.720 --> 00:58:41.250 align:middle line:90%
what?

00:58:41.250 --> 00:58:42.180 align:middle line:90%
Fragile, I guess.

00:58:42.180 --> 00:58:44.640 align:middle line:90%
So "kick the bucket" means die.

00:58:44.640 --> 00:58:48.870 align:middle line:84%
It also has a literal meaning
where you punt a pail.

00:58:48.870 --> 00:58:54.540 align:middle line:84%
But you can't change "kick"
or "bucket" with a synonym

00:58:54.540 --> 00:58:56.190 align:middle line:90%
and still mean "die."

00:58:56.190 --> 00:58:59.513 align:middle line:84%
So if he "punts the bucket,"
that doesn't mean he died.

00:58:59.513 --> 00:59:01.680 align:middle line:84%
It just means he literally
kicked the bucket, right?

00:59:01.680 --> 00:59:05.250 align:middle line:84%
Or if he "kicks a pail,"
if he "kicked the pail,"

00:59:05.250 --> 00:59:06.780 align:middle line:90%
that doesn't mean he died.

00:59:06.780 --> 00:59:10.680 align:middle line:84%
So it has to be
these words to have

00:59:10.680 --> 00:59:13.680 align:middle line:90%
it have its idiomatic meaning.

00:59:13.680 --> 00:59:14.790 align:middle line:90%
Idioms are fun.

00:59:14.790 --> 00:59:18.000 align:middle line:84%
If you get a chance, you can
ask your language consultant

00:59:18.000 --> 00:59:19.620 align:middle line:84%
whether they can
think of any idioms

00:59:19.620 --> 00:59:23.310 align:middle line:90%
that you can think about.

00:59:23.310 --> 00:59:24.460 align:middle line:90%
Now, here's the thing.

00:59:24.460 --> 00:59:27.180 align:middle line:84%
There are a zillion
idioms like these

00:59:27.180 --> 00:59:30.630 align:middle line:84%
where the idiom consists
of a verb and some phrase

00:59:30.630 --> 00:59:32.070 align:middle line:90%
that the verb combines with.

00:59:32.070 --> 00:59:34.745 align:middle line:84%
So a verb and its object, like
in the first three examples,

00:59:34.745 --> 00:59:36.120 align:middle line:84%
or a verb in
prepositional phrase

00:59:36.120 --> 00:59:37.230 align:middle line:90%
like in the last example--

00:59:37.230 --> 00:59:39.390 align:middle line:84%
lots and lots of
idioms like that.

00:59:39.390 --> 00:59:41.430 align:middle line:84%
So to say that
that's the idiom is

00:59:41.430 --> 00:59:43.840 align:middle line:84%
to say that if you have
this idiom in a sentence--

00:59:43.840 --> 00:59:47.010 align:middle line:84%
so if you say something like,
"John kicked the bucket,"

00:59:47.010 --> 00:59:48.720 align:middle line:84%
the idiomatic part
of that sentence

00:59:48.720 --> 00:59:49.980 align:middle line:90%
is "kick the bucket," right?

00:59:49.980 --> 00:59:52.170 align:middle line:90%
"John" is not part of the idiom.

00:59:52.170 --> 00:59:53.340 align:middle line:90%
Anybody can kick the bucket.

00:59:53.340 --> 00:59:54.945 align:middle line:90%
In fact, all of us will someday.

00:59:54.945 --> 00:59:56.880 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:59:56.880 --> 01:00:00.757 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, not to be
depressing or anything.

01:00:00.757 --> 01:00:02.340 align:middle line:84%
So the subject can
be anything at all.

01:00:02.340 --> 01:00:03.923 align:middle line:84%
It's the verb and
the object that make

01:00:03.923 --> 01:00:05.490 align:middle line:90%
the whole thing an idiom, yeah?

01:00:05.490 --> 01:00:07.140 align:middle line:84%
And then there's the
non-idiomatic part

01:00:07.140 --> 01:00:09.550 align:middle line:84%
that you add to the
idiom to make a sentence.

01:00:09.550 --> 01:00:11.350 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

01:00:11.350 --> 01:00:14.347 align:middle line:84%
So idioms are typically
parts of sentences

01:00:14.347 --> 01:00:16.930 align:middle line:84%
that combine with other things
to make complete sentences, not

01:00:16.930 --> 01:00:19.130 align:middle line:90%
other non-idiomatic stuff.

01:00:19.130 --> 01:00:22.180 align:middle line:84%
But here's the thing-- there
are restrictions on idioms.

01:00:22.180 --> 01:00:25.270 align:middle line:84%
There are no idioms that
consist of a subject and a verb

01:00:25.270 --> 01:00:27.800 align:middle line:84%
where the object
can be anything.

01:00:27.800 --> 01:00:30.130 align:middle line:84%
So I'm just giving
this as an example.

01:00:30.130 --> 01:00:32.990 align:middle line:84%
It's always hard to exemplify
this because, of course,

01:00:32.990 --> 01:00:35.200 align:middle line:84%
most strings of
words are not idioms.

01:00:35.200 --> 01:00:39.637 align:middle line:84%
But the last slide showed you,
there are a zillion idioms

01:00:39.637 --> 01:00:41.470 align:middle line:84%
where the verb and the
object are an idiom--

01:00:41.470 --> 01:00:43.760 align:middle line:84%
"kick the bucket,"
"buy the farm"--

01:00:43.760 --> 01:00:46.820 align:middle line:84%
but there are no idioms where
it's the subject and the verb.

01:00:46.820 --> 01:00:50.290 align:middle line:84%
So here's an example
of a non-idiom.

01:00:50.290 --> 01:00:53.620 align:middle line:84%
There isn't an idiom, "the
armadillo bit (blank)."

01:00:53.620 --> 01:00:55.930 align:middle line:84%
So you can imagine
an idiom where

01:00:55.930 --> 01:00:58.810 align:middle line:84%
if I said, "The armadillo
bit John yesterday,"

01:00:58.810 --> 01:01:03.260 align:middle line:84%
it meant "John was very busy
yesterday" or something.

01:01:03.260 --> 01:01:06.850 align:middle line:84%
Or, "The armadillo
bit me this morning"

01:01:06.850 --> 01:01:08.832 align:middle line:84%
means I have a
headache, or something.

01:01:08.832 --> 01:01:10.790 align:middle line:84%
You can imagine it meaning
something like that.

01:01:10.790 --> 01:01:12.010 align:middle line:90%
But that is not an idiom.

01:01:12.010 --> 01:01:14.555 align:middle line:84%
And more importantly, there
are no idioms like that.

01:01:14.555 --> 01:01:15.055 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

01:01:15.055 --> 01:01:18.430 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What about like
"The cat got your tongue"?

01:01:18.430 --> 01:01:21.700 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Well,
that's a nice example.

01:01:21.700 --> 01:01:24.340 align:middle line:84%
Notice, though, that the object
is part of the idiom, right?

01:01:24.340 --> 01:01:32.980 align:middle line:84%
So actually, "Cat got
(blank)'s tongue."

01:01:32.980 --> 01:01:36.130 align:middle line:84%
Actually, can the possessor of
the tongue be anyone at all?

01:01:36.130 --> 01:01:39.460 align:middle line:84%
Can I say, "The cat
got John's tongue?"

01:01:39.460 --> 01:01:42.490 align:middle line:84%
Meaning John had a hard time
thinking of what to say?

01:01:42.490 --> 01:01:44.260 align:middle line:84%
I think the easiest
way for me to use

01:01:44.260 --> 01:01:48.190 align:middle line:84%
this idiom is to ask someone,
"Cat got your tongue?"

01:01:48.190 --> 01:01:51.400 align:middle line:84%
If I have a hard time coming
up with something to say,

01:01:51.400 --> 01:01:54.540 align:middle line:84%
can I say "The cat
got my tongue?"

01:01:54.540 --> 01:01:56.250 align:middle line:84%
So there may or may
not be a blank here.

01:01:56.250 --> 01:01:58.080 align:middle line:90%
Maybe there should be "or" here.

01:01:58.080 --> 01:02:00.600 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, the subject is
indeed part of the idiom,

01:02:00.600 --> 01:02:02.110 align:middle line:90%
but so is the object.

01:02:02.110 --> 01:02:04.440 align:middle line:90%
So this is a nice--

01:02:04.440 --> 01:02:07.320 align:middle line:84%
you're making me refine what I
just said, which is important.

01:02:07.320 --> 01:02:09.520 align:middle line:84%
It's not the idioms can't
contain the subject.

01:02:09.520 --> 01:02:10.020 align:middle line:90%
They can.

01:02:10.020 --> 01:02:11.250 align:middle line:90%
This is a nice example.

01:02:11.250 --> 01:02:13.960 align:middle line:84%
But they also have to
contain the object.

01:02:13.960 --> 01:02:14.460 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

01:02:14.460 --> 01:02:17.200 align:middle line:84%
What if [INAUDIBLE]
something like "his tongue."

01:02:17.200 --> 01:02:18.770 align:middle line:90%
The cat got his tongue--

01:02:18.770 --> 01:02:19.720 align:middle line:90%
can you say that?

01:02:19.720 --> 01:02:23.020 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

01:02:23.020 --> 01:02:25.540 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

01:02:25.540 --> 01:02:26.280 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:02:26.280 --> 01:02:27.610 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, my son--

01:02:27.610 --> 01:02:29.943 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: That's when he's
like, what's wrong with this?

01:02:29.943 --> 01:02:30.640 align:middle line:90%


01:02:30.640 --> 01:02:32.140 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Cat got his tongue.

01:02:32.140 --> 01:02:35.680 align:middle line:84%
My son, who's 11,
is shy, and he's

01:02:35.680 --> 01:02:38.140 align:middle line:84%
having a hard time
saying anything in front

01:02:38.140 --> 01:02:39.430 align:middle line:90%
of people he doesn't know.

01:02:39.430 --> 01:02:41.772 align:middle line:84%
And I say, "Oh, the
cat got his tongue."

01:02:41.772 --> 01:02:43.710 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

01:02:43.710 --> 01:02:46.110 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Maybe,
yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:02:46.110 --> 01:02:48.508 align:middle line:84%
So and this is why
I waffled about

01:02:48.508 --> 01:02:49.800 align:middle line:90%
whether there was a blank here.

01:02:49.800 --> 01:02:52.180 align:middle line:84%
The fact that there can be
a blank here is interesting.

01:02:52.180 --> 01:02:53.075 align:middle line:90%
But the point is--

01:02:53.075 --> 01:02:54.450 align:middle line:84%
there are several
points to make.

01:02:54.450 --> 01:02:57.510 align:middle line:84%
But one is, look, "tongue"
is also part of this idiom.

01:02:57.510 --> 01:02:59.280 align:middle line:84%
So the object is
part of the idiom.

01:02:59.280 --> 01:03:02.490 align:middle line:84%
You're absolutely right that
you might have some options

01:03:02.490 --> 01:03:03.840 align:middle line:90%
about what to put here.

01:03:03.840 --> 01:03:04.590 align:middle line:90%
Raquel?

01:03:04.590 --> 01:03:08.510 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I feel like if you
just have a, quote, "idiom,"

01:03:08.510 --> 01:03:09.435 align:middle line:90%
it's just like a verb.

01:03:09.435 --> 01:03:10.310 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yep.

01:03:10.310 --> 01:03:11.390 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And then,
if you just say

01:03:11.390 --> 01:03:13.010 align:middle line:84%
that that's an
alternative meaning,

01:03:13.010 --> 01:03:16.820 align:middle line:84%
or like a slang meaning of
that verb, like "flame," like,

01:03:16.820 --> 01:03:17.630 align:middle line:90%
get really angry--

01:03:17.630 --> 01:03:17.990 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Flame.

01:03:17.990 --> 01:03:18.860 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: --or
something like that.

01:03:18.860 --> 01:03:20.510 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, people don't
call it an idiom.

01:03:20.510 --> 01:03:23.110 align:middle line:84%
They just say, oh, yeah,
"flame" is just [INAUDIBLE]..

01:03:23.110 --> 01:03:26.600 align:middle line:90%


01:03:26.600 --> 01:03:28.738 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
That's a nice example.

01:03:28.738 --> 01:03:30.530 align:middle line:84%
So maybe this is a way
to think about this.

01:03:30.530 --> 01:03:33.072 align:middle line:84%
If you're thinking about a tree
here-- we'll put a tree here.

01:03:33.072 --> 01:03:39.120 align:middle line:90%


01:03:39.120 --> 01:03:42.140 align:middle line:84%
So what we're seeing
is there are idioms--

01:03:42.140 --> 01:03:43.913 align:middle line:84%
I started by saying
there idioms like this

01:03:43.913 --> 01:03:45.830 align:middle line:84%
where this whole thing
is the idiom-- the verb

01:03:45.830 --> 01:03:48.325 align:middle line:84%
and a noun phrase after
it like, "buy the farm."

01:03:48.325 --> 01:03:49.700 align:middle line:84%
Raquel is raising
the possibility

01:03:49.700 --> 01:03:51.367 align:middle line:84%
that there could be
idioms that are just

01:03:51.367 --> 01:03:53.600 align:middle line:84%
this size, although, as
you say, that's not usually

01:03:53.600 --> 01:03:54.830 align:middle line:90%
what you call them.

01:03:54.830 --> 01:03:58.550 align:middle line:84%
So maybe "flame," where
the subject and the object

01:03:58.550 --> 01:03:59.690 align:middle line:90%
are not part of that idiom.

01:03:59.690 --> 01:04:01.790 align:middle line:84%
So I can flame you,
you can flame me,

01:04:01.790 --> 01:04:02.840 align:middle line:90%
anyone can flame anyone.

01:04:02.840 --> 01:04:03.950 align:middle line:90%
Those are not part of the idiom.

01:04:03.950 --> 01:04:05.560 align:middle line:84%
The idiom is just
the verb, "flame."

01:04:05.560 --> 01:04:09.930 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so yeah, we could
call that an idiom or not.

01:04:09.930 --> 01:04:13.612 align:middle line:84%
And then, earlier pointed
out, "cat got your tongue,"

01:04:13.612 --> 01:04:15.320 align:middle line:84%
there are bigger idioms
where the subject

01:04:15.320 --> 01:04:17.570 align:middle line:84%
and the verb and the object
are all part of the idiom.

01:04:17.570 --> 01:04:19.670 align:middle line:84%
And then, there's
questions about how do you

01:04:19.670 --> 01:04:21.710 align:middle line:90%
get a possessor into here?

01:04:21.710 --> 01:04:23.010 align:middle line:90%
I'm trying to think about that.

01:04:23.010 --> 01:04:25.510 align:middle line:84%
So a way to talk about this,
then, is to say, yeah, idioms--

01:04:25.510 --> 01:04:27.020 align:middle line:90%
but the point that I'm making--

01:04:27.020 --> 01:04:28.520 align:middle line:84%
attempting to make--
is there aren't

01:04:28.520 --> 01:04:31.070 align:middle line:90%
any idioms that are just this.

01:04:31.070 --> 01:04:33.320 align:middle line:84%
I wish I had colored
chalk or something.

01:04:33.320 --> 01:04:35.330 align:middle line:84%
There aren't any idioms that are
just the subject and the verb,

01:04:35.330 --> 01:04:36.930 align:middle line:84%
and the object can
be anything at all.

01:04:36.930 --> 01:04:39.020 align:middle line:84%
There's no "The
armadillo bit John."

01:04:39.020 --> 01:04:43.820 align:middle line:84%
There's no "The kumquats
have fooled Mary," right?

01:04:43.820 --> 01:04:46.280 align:middle line:84%
Where "The kumquats
have fooled Mary"

01:04:46.280 --> 01:04:50.120 align:middle line:90%
means Mary is daydreaming.

01:04:50.120 --> 01:04:52.490 align:middle line:90%
There aren't idioms like that.

01:04:52.490 --> 01:04:53.300 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:04:53.300 --> 01:04:56.195 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So is it basically
like it has to be [INAUDIBLE]??

01:04:56.195 --> 01:04:58.070 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: It's
looking like it, right?

01:04:58.070 --> 01:04:59.600 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, something like that.

01:04:59.600 --> 01:05:03.680 align:middle line:84%
Although, how you're going
to get "his" or "your" here

01:05:03.680 --> 01:05:07.010 align:middle line:84%
in "the cat got your tongue,"
that's an interesting question,

01:05:07.010 --> 01:05:09.980 align:middle line:84%
because that's presumably
part of this noun phrase.

01:05:09.980 --> 01:05:13.592 align:middle line:84%
But sweeping that potential
problem under the rug,

01:05:13.592 --> 01:05:15.050 align:middle line:84%
yeah, it's just,
[? those ?] idioms

01:05:15.050 --> 01:05:18.350 align:middle line:90%
have to be constituents, yeah.

01:05:18.350 --> 01:05:20.120 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:05:20.120 --> 01:05:22.250 align:middle line:90%
OK?

01:05:22.250 --> 01:05:24.680 align:middle line:84%
I'm pausing here
because here I am boldly

01:05:24.680 --> 01:05:26.160 align:middle line:84%
asserting there
is no-- of course,

01:05:26.160 --> 01:05:27.785 align:middle line:84%
there is no idiom,
"the armadillo bit."

01:05:27.785 --> 01:05:29.750 align:middle line:84%
But if you pick any
random verb and noun,

01:05:29.750 --> 01:05:32.060 align:middle line:90%
it's probably not an idiom.

01:05:32.060 --> 01:05:35.600 align:middle line:84%
So there also isn't an idiom,
"bit the armadillo," right?

01:05:35.600 --> 01:05:37.745 align:middle line:84%
That could have been
an idiom, but it isn't.

01:05:37.745 --> 01:05:39.620 align:middle line:84%
So "John bit the
armadillo last night"

01:05:39.620 --> 01:05:42.440 align:middle line:84%
that doesn't mean "John
worked hard last night."

01:05:42.440 --> 01:05:44.990 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, it could have,
but it doesn't.

01:05:44.990 --> 01:05:47.820 align:middle line:84%
So claims of this kind
are always a little weird.

01:05:47.820 --> 01:05:50.815 align:middle line:84%
You should all go home
and try to think, annoy

01:05:50.815 --> 01:05:52.190 align:middle line:84%
your friends, and
your relatives,

01:05:52.190 --> 01:05:55.100 align:middle line:84%
and your roommates trying
to come up with idioms

01:05:55.100 --> 01:05:56.040 align:middle line:90%
that have this shape.

01:05:56.040 --> 01:05:59.440 align:middle line:90%
But there don't seem to be any.

01:05:59.440 --> 01:05:59.940 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:05:59.940 --> 01:06:00.390 align:middle line:90%
OK, good.

01:06:00.390 --> 01:06:01.770 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to move
quickly to the next slide

01:06:01.770 --> 01:06:03.187 align:middle line:84%
before somebody
comes up with one.

01:06:03.187 --> 01:06:03.883 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:06:03.883 --> 01:06:06.300 align:middle line:84%
This isn't because the subject
can't be part of the idiom.

01:06:06.300 --> 01:06:07.200 align:middle line:90%
This has already come up.

01:06:07.200 --> 01:06:09.000 align:middle line:84%
So "The cat got your tongue,"
"The cat is out of the bag,"

01:06:09.000 --> 01:06:11.620 align:middle line:84%
"The shit will hit the fan,"
there are lots of idioms.

01:06:11.620 --> 01:06:14.085 align:middle line:84%
So "The shit will hit the
fan" has a literal meaning.

01:06:14.085 --> 01:06:14.790 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:06:14.790 --> 01:06:17.130 align:middle line:84%
But it also has a
figurative meaning,

01:06:17.130 --> 01:06:20.250 align:middle line:84%
like the situation will get
dramatically worse quickly,

01:06:20.250 --> 01:06:21.490 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

01:06:21.490 --> 01:06:22.650 align:middle line:90%
That's what it means.

01:06:22.650 --> 01:06:24.317 align:middle line:84%
"The cat is out of
the bag," yeah, there

01:06:24.317 --> 01:06:26.010 align:middle line:84%
could be a literal
cat in a literal bag,

01:06:26.010 --> 01:06:28.020 align:middle line:84%
or it could mean
the secret is out.

01:06:28.020 --> 01:06:29.820 align:middle line:84%
That's the other
thing it can mean.

01:06:29.820 --> 01:06:32.280 align:middle line:84%
There are many idioms
about cats for some reason.

01:06:32.280 --> 01:06:35.850 align:middle line:90%


01:06:35.850 --> 01:06:38.290 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, there are idioms
like "John will buy the farm."

01:06:38.290 --> 01:06:40.373 align:middle line:84%
There are idioms like "The
shit will hit the fan."

01:06:40.373 --> 01:06:41.630 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's what we've seen.

01:06:41.630 --> 01:06:44.130 align:middle line:84%
And there are no idioms like
"The armadillo will bite John."

01:06:44.130 --> 01:06:45.323 align:middle line:90%
Good.

01:06:45.323 --> 01:06:47.365 align:middle line:84%
Maybe it's as though idioms
must be constituents.

01:06:47.365 --> 01:06:50.990 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, nicely,
nicely pointed out.

01:06:50.990 --> 01:06:55.320 align:middle line:84%
So OK, good, now we know
something about idioms.

01:06:55.320 --> 01:06:59.420 align:middle line:84%
So "The shit hit the
fan," it's a fine idiom.

01:06:59.420 --> 01:07:01.530 align:middle line:84%
"The shit seemed
to hit the fan,"

01:07:01.530 --> 01:07:04.530 align:middle line:84%
"The shit seemed to be
likely to hit the fan,"

01:07:04.530 --> 01:07:07.740 align:middle line:84%
these are all fine, and they
all have idiomatic readings.

01:07:07.740 --> 01:07:10.630 align:middle line:90%


01:07:10.630 --> 01:07:12.510 align:middle line:84%
But the things
that I have circled

01:07:12.510 --> 01:07:15.660 align:middle line:84%
are surely not
constituents, right?

01:07:15.660 --> 01:07:18.120 align:middle line:84%
So "The shit hit the
fan" is a constituent,

01:07:18.120 --> 01:07:20.160 align:middle line:84%
but "The shit seemed to
hit the fan"-- "seem to"

01:07:20.160 --> 01:07:21.450 align:middle line:90%
is not part of the idiom.

01:07:21.450 --> 01:07:24.780 align:middle line:84%
These sentences are OK, and they
have their idiomatic meanings.

01:07:24.780 --> 01:07:28.770 align:middle line:84%
"Everything was fine all day
until suddenly, at 12:35,

01:07:28.770 --> 01:07:31.140 align:middle line:84%
the shit seemed to
hit the fan"-- fine.

01:07:31.140 --> 01:07:33.120 align:middle line:90%
Sad, of course, but grammatical.

01:07:33.120 --> 01:07:35.670 align:middle line:90%


01:07:35.670 --> 01:07:38.550 align:middle line:84%
But "seem to" is not
part of the idiom.

01:07:38.550 --> 01:07:40.560 align:middle line:84%
It's like John in
"John bought the farm."

01:07:40.560 --> 01:07:43.170 align:middle line:84%
The idiom is, "The
shit hit the fan."

01:07:43.170 --> 01:07:45.330 align:middle line:84%
Or "The shit hit the
fan" in "The shit seemed

01:07:45.330 --> 01:07:46.860 align:middle line:90%
to be likely to hit the fan."

01:07:46.860 --> 01:07:50.550 align:middle line:84%
So "I walked into the
office, and everything

01:07:50.550 --> 01:07:51.510 align:middle line:90%
seemed very precarious.

01:07:51.510 --> 01:07:52.280 align:middle line:90%
Tempers were high.

01:07:52.280 --> 01:07:54.780 align:middle line:84%
The shit seemed to be likely
to hit the fan at any minute"--

01:07:54.780 --> 01:07:56.130 align:middle line:90%
yeah, perfectly fine.

01:07:56.130 --> 01:07:57.870 align:middle line:90%
Has the idiomatic reading.

01:07:57.870 --> 01:08:01.580 align:middle line:84%
But these things are quite
far apart from each other.

01:08:01.580 --> 01:08:04.000 align:middle line:84%
But if we believe
in NP-movement,

01:08:04.000 --> 01:08:05.650 align:middle line:90%
then we have a story.

01:08:05.650 --> 01:08:07.010 align:middle line:90%
The story goes like this.

01:08:07.010 --> 01:08:09.160 align:middle line:84%
How do you say, "The shit
seemed to hit the fan"?

01:08:09.160 --> 01:08:11.290 align:middle line:84%
Well, yeah, "the shit"
and "hit the fan"

01:08:11.290 --> 01:08:14.320 align:middle line:84%
are far apart from each
other when you're done,

01:08:14.320 --> 01:08:18.520 align:middle line:84%
but you start off with "seemed,
the shit to hit the fan,"

01:08:18.520 --> 01:08:22.689 align:middle line:84%
and then "the shit" moved
into the specifier of TP

01:08:22.689 --> 01:08:24.010 align:middle line:90%
in the higher clause.

01:08:24.010 --> 01:08:26.800 align:middle line:84%
That subject moves up to become
the subject of the higher

01:08:26.800 --> 01:08:28.210 align:middle line:90%
thing because of the EPP.

01:08:28.210 --> 01:08:31.870 align:middle line:84%
The EPP wants there to
be a subject up there.

01:08:31.870 --> 01:08:34.450 align:middle line:84%
Another option would be to put
an expletive there and say,

01:08:34.450 --> 01:08:36.850 align:middle line:84%
"It seemed that the
shit had hit the fan,"

01:08:36.850 --> 01:08:38.308 align:middle line:84%
or whatever-- to
put an "it" there.

01:08:38.308 --> 01:08:39.767 align:middle line:84%
But if you're not
going to do that,

01:08:39.767 --> 01:08:42.310 align:middle line:84%
you need to move the subject
of the lower clause up

01:08:42.310 --> 01:08:43.810 align:middle line:90%
into the higher clause.

01:08:43.810 --> 01:08:46.420 align:middle line:84%
The consequence of that is
that by the time you're done,

01:08:46.420 --> 01:08:48.819 align:middle line:84%
"The shit hit the fan" is
no longer a constituent.

01:08:48.819 --> 01:08:52.779 align:middle line:84%
But it was once, and
apparently, that's what matters.

01:08:52.779 --> 01:08:54.642 align:middle line:84%
It needs to start
as a constituent.

01:08:54.642 --> 01:08:56.350 align:middle line:84%
Maybe one way to think
about it would be,

01:08:56.350 --> 01:09:00.370 align:middle line:84%
the idiom is a really big
and complicated lexical item.

01:09:00.370 --> 01:09:03.154 align:middle line:84%
If you're going to merge it, you
need to merge it all at once.

01:09:03.154 --> 01:09:04.779 align:middle line:84%
It's one way to think
about it, anyway.

01:09:04.779 --> 01:09:05.950 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:09:05.950 --> 01:09:07.115 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, question.

01:09:07.115 --> 01:09:07.580 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

01:09:07.580 --> 01:09:09.122 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So first
of all, what would

01:09:09.122 --> 01:09:11.060 align:middle line:84%
happen if you wanted
to say, "The shit

01:09:11.060 --> 01:09:12.529 align:middle line:90%
was going to hit the fan?"

01:09:12.529 --> 01:09:15.450 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Oh, good question.

01:09:15.450 --> 01:09:20.989 align:middle line:90%
So "The shit hit the fan."

01:09:20.989 --> 01:09:24.149 align:middle line:90%


01:09:24.149 --> 01:09:26.600 align:middle line:90%
"The shit--" very nice point.

01:09:26.600 --> 01:09:27.770 align:middle line:90%
"The shit will hit the fan."

01:09:27.770 --> 01:09:30.720 align:middle line:90%


01:09:30.720 --> 01:09:33.060 align:middle line:90%
"The shit might hit the fan."

01:09:33.060 --> 01:09:37.380 align:middle line:84%
"The shit is going to hit
the fan" Yeah, there are lots

01:09:37.380 --> 01:09:38.630 align:middle line:90%
of things you can put in here.

01:09:38.630 --> 01:09:42.109 align:middle line:90%


01:09:42.109 --> 01:09:44.880 align:middle line:84%
There is-- that a
very nice point.

01:09:44.880 --> 01:09:46.880 align:middle line:84%
There is a classic response
to this point, which

01:09:46.880 --> 01:09:50.090 align:middle line:84%
is to say, yeah, what you've
discovered is there's even more

01:09:50.090 --> 01:09:52.010 align:middle line:90%
NP-movement than we thought.

01:09:52.010 --> 01:09:57.617 align:middle line:84%
So if I-- let me get a
fresh board down here.

01:09:57.617 --> 01:09:58.700 align:middle line:90%
How are we doing for time?

01:09:58.700 --> 01:10:02.180 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, this would be a good
place to stop, probably.

01:10:02.180 --> 01:10:04.400 align:middle line:84%
The way I have been
drawing trees for you,

01:10:04.400 --> 01:10:18.840 align:middle line:84%
I've been drawing trees like,
"John will eat the kumquats."

01:10:18.840 --> 01:10:21.480 align:middle line:90%


01:10:21.480 --> 01:10:23.790 align:middle line:84%
I've been drawing
trees like this, yeah?

01:10:23.790 --> 01:10:24.870 align:middle line:90%
Does this look familiar?

01:10:24.870 --> 01:10:26.870 align:middle line:84%
Does anybody have any
questions about this tree?

01:10:26.870 --> 01:10:29.400 align:middle line:84%
This should be
painless at this point.

01:10:29.400 --> 01:10:31.950 align:middle line:84%
If it's painful, then
you should stop me.

01:10:31.950 --> 01:10:33.420 align:middle line:90%
Is this OK?

01:10:33.420 --> 01:10:35.457 align:middle line:84%
So we've got a verb
phrase that consists

01:10:35.457 --> 01:10:37.290 align:middle line:84%
of a verb and an object,
"eat the kumquats."

01:10:37.290 --> 01:10:39.280 align:middle line:84%
"Eat" is selecting
for the object.

01:10:39.280 --> 01:10:42.090 align:middle line:84%
And then we've said
that words like "will"

01:10:42.090 --> 01:10:49.148 align:middle line:84%
are instances of T, tense, and
that T is the phrase, whose

01:10:49.148 --> 01:10:50.940 align:middle line:84%
specifier, the subjects
it's in-- actually,

01:10:50.940 --> 01:10:53.160 align:middle line:84%
we have a name now for the fact
that the subject sits there.

01:10:53.160 --> 01:10:54.868 align:middle line:84%
That's the Extended
Projection Principle.

01:10:54.868 --> 01:10:55.750 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:10:55.750 --> 01:10:57.292 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
And we've said there

01:10:57.292 --> 01:11:00.930 align:middle line:84%
is some force that demands
that there be something here.

01:11:00.930 --> 01:11:03.660 align:middle line:84%
That's part of
why John was here.

01:11:03.660 --> 01:11:06.390 align:middle line:84%
Your point, which is
a very well-taken one,

01:11:06.390 --> 01:11:12.480 align:middle line:84%
is that if I say, "The
shit will hit the fan--"

01:11:12.480 --> 01:11:14.310 align:middle line:84%
remind me to erase
this before we leave--

01:11:14.310 --> 01:11:23.410 align:middle line:90%


01:11:23.410 --> 01:11:26.560 align:middle line:84%
I've been going quickly
partly in the hope

01:11:26.560 --> 01:11:29.110 align:middle line:84%
that nobody would notice
this, but yes, problem.

01:11:29.110 --> 01:11:33.070 align:middle line:84%
This and this seemed to be
the constituent, the idiom,

01:11:33.070 --> 01:11:36.220 align:middle line:84%
but "will" is not
part of the idiom.

01:11:36.220 --> 01:11:38.860 align:middle line:84%
And there's a classic way of
dealing with this problem.

01:11:38.860 --> 01:11:40.943 align:middle line:84%
This is one of the classic
arguments for something

01:11:40.943 --> 01:11:42.820 align:middle line:84%
that is now widely
believed, which

01:11:42.820 --> 01:11:46.450 align:middle line:84%
is that the subject, although
it is in the specifier of TP,

01:11:46.450 --> 01:11:48.340 align:middle line:90%
it doesn't actually start there.

01:11:48.340 --> 01:11:50.350 align:middle line:84%
I've been drawing trees
where it starts there,

01:11:50.350 --> 01:11:51.640 align:middle line:90%
but it actually starts lower.

01:11:51.640 --> 01:11:53.350 align:middle line:84%
It starts inside
the verb phrase,

01:11:53.350 --> 01:11:54.790 align:middle line:90%
and it moves up to here.

01:11:54.790 --> 01:11:56.054 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]?

01:11:56.054 --> 01:12:00.140 align:middle line:90%


01:12:00.140 --> 01:12:02.780 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, so that
the real derivation for this--

01:12:02.780 --> 01:12:04.613 align:middle line:84%
this is what I've been
concealing from you--

01:12:04.613 --> 01:12:12.320 align:middle line:84%
but the real derivation from
this starts with the subject

01:12:12.320 --> 01:12:13.430 align:middle line:90%
inside the verb phrase--

01:12:13.430 --> 01:12:16.010 align:middle line:84%
I would say it's a specifier
of the verb phrase--

01:12:16.010 --> 01:12:20.240 align:middle line:84%
and it is raising to the
specifier of TP always.

01:12:20.240 --> 01:12:23.630 align:middle line:84%
So I've been talking so far as
though the specifier or TP--

01:12:23.630 --> 01:12:25.040 align:middle line:84%
we've talked now
about some cases

01:12:25.040 --> 01:12:27.860 align:middle line:84%
where something moves
into the specifier of TP.

01:12:27.860 --> 01:12:31.760 align:middle line:84%
But from the earlier kinds of
trees I was drawing for you,

01:12:31.760 --> 01:12:34.250 align:middle line:84%
I was making it sound like the
specifier of TP, sometimes,

01:12:34.250 --> 01:12:35.542 align:middle line:90%
something is just merged there.

01:12:35.542 --> 01:12:36.950 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't move there.

01:12:36.950 --> 01:12:39.347 align:middle line:84%
For exactly the reason that
you've just pointed out,

01:12:39.347 --> 01:12:41.930 align:middle line:84%
people think that that's wrong--
that really, what's happening

01:12:41.930 --> 01:12:44.750 align:middle line:84%
is that the subject always
starts lower than TP

01:12:44.750 --> 01:12:46.857 align:middle line:84%
and raises into the
specifier of TP.

01:12:46.857 --> 01:12:49.190 align:middle line:84%
And in English, it has to
raise into the specifier of TP

01:12:49.190 --> 01:12:50.870 align:middle line:90%
because of the EPP.

01:12:50.870 --> 01:12:53.180 align:middle line:84%
And this argument from
the behavior of idioms,

01:12:53.180 --> 01:12:56.040 align:middle line:84%
this is one of the classic
arguments for that conclusion,

01:12:56.040 --> 01:12:56.540 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

01:12:56.540 --> 01:12:59.120 align:middle line:84%
This is something
that was discovered

01:12:59.120 --> 01:13:03.080 align:middle line:84%
in the late '80s people
first started saying that.

01:13:03.080 --> 01:13:09.770 align:middle line:84%
It's called the VP internal
subject hypothesis.

01:13:09.770 --> 01:13:13.190 align:middle line:84%
And by now, it's very
widely assumed to be true.

01:13:13.190 --> 01:13:15.890 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, I was planning to conceal
this from you because this is

01:13:15.890 --> 01:13:19.190 align:middle line:84%
24.900, but I should know better
than to try to conceal things

01:13:19.190 --> 01:13:19.790 align:middle line:90%
from you guys.

01:13:19.790 --> 01:13:21.410 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, does that
answer your question?

01:13:21.410 --> 01:13:22.035 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:13:22.035 --> 01:13:23.660 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Cool.

01:13:23.660 --> 01:13:26.630 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is this an example
about knowledge theory, where

01:13:26.630 --> 01:13:28.990 align:middle line:84%
it's like we wanted idiom
to be a constituent,

01:13:28.990 --> 01:13:32.060 align:middle line:84%
but it matters more
to satisfy the EPP?

01:13:32.060 --> 01:13:33.680 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: No.

01:13:33.680 --> 01:13:35.035 align:middle line:90%
I see what you mean.

01:13:35.035 --> 01:13:37.160 align:middle line:84%
The way people standardly
think about this, anyway,

01:13:37.160 --> 01:13:38.350 align:middle line:90%
it's something more like--

01:13:38.350 --> 01:13:42.350 align:middle line:90%


01:13:42.350 --> 01:13:44.460 align:middle line:84%
you're raising an
interesting question.

01:13:44.460 --> 01:13:46.910 align:middle line:84%
It would be interesting to
think of ways of distinguishing

01:13:46.910 --> 01:13:50.030 align:middle line:90%
these theories from each other.

01:13:50.030 --> 01:13:53.750 align:middle line:84%
The way people think about
this, it's something more like--

01:13:53.750 --> 01:13:56.230 align:middle line:90%
it's sort of-- mm.

01:13:56.230 --> 01:13:58.020 align:middle line:84%
Remember the
Projection Principle?

01:13:58.020 --> 01:14:00.777 align:middle line:84%
So in the Projection Principle,
it says the verb absolutely,

01:14:00.777 --> 01:14:03.360 align:middle line:84%
absolutely has to have a sister
if it's transitive, let's say.

01:14:03.360 --> 01:14:05.490 align:middle line:84%
It has to have a noun
phrase as its sister.

01:14:05.490 --> 01:14:09.390 align:middle line:84%
And then, there are these other
forces, things like wh-movement

01:14:09.390 --> 01:14:11.250 align:middle line:84%
that say, oh, but
wait, I want this thing

01:14:11.250 --> 01:14:12.670 align:middle line:90%
to be over here instead.

01:14:12.670 --> 01:14:17.440 align:middle line:84%
And so we say, yeah, it starts
here, and then it moves.

01:14:17.440 --> 01:14:20.370 align:middle line:84%
So it's as though there are
these mutually incompatible

01:14:20.370 --> 01:14:23.647 align:middle line:84%
requirements which are met
by having a derivation,

01:14:23.647 --> 01:14:25.230 align:middle line:84%
where something
starts in one position

01:14:25.230 --> 01:14:26.970 align:middle line:84%
and then moves to
another position.

01:14:26.970 --> 01:14:29.100 align:middle line:84%
And the idea is that
the Projection Principle

01:14:29.100 --> 01:14:32.250 align:middle line:84%
is satisfied because, well,
you satisfied it first,

01:14:32.250 --> 01:14:36.840 align:middle line:84%
at the beginning, and then
later you did other things.

01:14:36.840 --> 01:14:39.000 align:middle line:90%
We're going to talk more--

01:14:39.000 --> 01:14:41.460 align:middle line:84%
I think I promised that we
would only do another day or two

01:14:41.460 --> 01:14:45.435 align:middle line:84%
of syntax, so I'm
running out of room

01:14:45.435 --> 01:14:47.560 align:middle line:84%
in which to promise that
we will talk about things.

01:14:47.560 --> 01:14:49.393 align:middle line:84%
But I think we'll have
a chance to talk more

01:14:49.393 --> 01:14:51.198 align:middle line:84%
about the nature
of the derivation

01:14:51.198 --> 01:14:52.740 align:middle line:84%
and the order in
which you do things,

01:14:52.740 --> 01:14:54.990 align:middle line:84%
like the order in which
you have to satisfy things.

01:14:54.990 --> 01:14:57.365 align:middle line:84%
But the standard way people
talk about this kind of thing

01:14:57.365 --> 01:14:59.370 align:middle line:84%
is more like the
rule-ordering kinds of things

01:14:59.370 --> 01:15:00.037 align:middle line:90%
we talked about.

01:15:00.037 --> 01:15:02.748 align:middle line:84%
It's like there is an early
stage at which you must obey

01:15:02.748 --> 01:15:04.290 align:middle line:84%
the Projection
Principle, or at which

01:15:04.290 --> 01:15:06.390 align:middle line:84%
you must obey this
condition on idioms,

01:15:06.390 --> 01:15:08.970 align:middle line:84%
and then there are other
things that come along and mess

01:15:08.970 --> 01:15:10.530 align:middle line:90%
everything up subsequently.

01:15:10.530 --> 01:15:14.050 align:middle line:84%
That's a way people
talk about this.

01:15:14.050 --> 01:15:20.260 align:middle line:84%
Any questions about
idioms, about constituents?

01:15:20.260 --> 01:15:21.335 align:middle line:90%
Anything else?

01:15:21.335 --> 01:15:22.580 align:middle line:90%
NP-movement?

01:15:22.580 --> 01:15:23.830 align:middle line:90%
Awesome.

01:15:23.830 --> 01:15:26.440 align:middle line:90%
So let me make sure--

01:15:26.440 --> 01:15:28.665 align:middle line:84%
yes, this is an
excellent place to stop.

01:15:28.665 --> 01:15:30.790 align:middle line:84%
So let me ask again, are
there any other questions?

01:15:30.790 --> 01:15:33.350 align:middle line:90%
Or shall we just stop?

01:15:33.350 --> 01:15:34.490 align:middle line:90%
Cool, good.

01:15:34.490 --> 01:15:36.980 align:middle line:84%
Now you know slightly more than
I was planning to teach you

01:15:36.980 --> 01:15:38.750 align:middle line:90%
about the behavior of subjects.

01:15:38.750 --> 01:15:42.070 align:middle line:84%
And we'll pick it
up here next time.

01:15:42.070 --> 01:15:46.000 align:middle line:90%